// ============================================================
// WEATHER TEST DATA — q: question, o: [options a-d], a: correct index,
// r: rationale, ref: topic + enabling objective.
// Source: NIFE Aviation Weather Practice Tests 1-3 (SG Jan 2025,
// CIN Q-9B-0178), incl. their answer keys & reasoning.
// ============================================================
const TESTS = [
{
  name: "Test 1 \u2014 Easy",
  desc: "Single-concept recall drawn directly from Student Guide language.",
  questions: [
    {q:"Which layer of the atmosphere is adjacent to the Earth's surface and contains nearly all weather?", o:["Stratosphere", "Ionosphere", "Tropopause", "Troposphere"], a:3, r:"Troposphere. SG, The Atmosphere / Troposphere: \"The troposphere is the layer adjacent to the Earth's surface\u2026 nearly all weather occurs here. \" The tropopause (C) is only the transition zone at its top, not a weather layer.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.172"},
    {q:"Pure, dry air in the atmosphere is composed of approximately:", o:["21% nitrogen, 78% oxygen, 1% other gases", "50% nitrogen, 50% oxygen", "78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, 1% other gases", "90% oxygen, 9% nitrogen, 1% other gases"], a:2, r:"78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, 1% other. SG, Composition: \"Pure, dry air contains 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, a 1% mixture of ten other gases. \" Note that water vapor (0\u20135% by volume) is separate from dry air.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.172"},
    {q:"Atmospheric (barometric) pressure is best defined as:", o:["The density of the air at a given altitude", "The pressure exerted on a surface by the weight of the column of air directly above it", "The temperature of the air corrected for humidity", "The rate at which pressure changes with distance"], a:1, r:"SG, Atmospheric Pressure: \"Atmospheric (barometric) pressure is the pressure exerted on a surface by the atmosphere due to the weight of the column of air directly above that surface. \" D describes the pressure gradient, not pressure.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.173"},
    {q:"Which two units are used in the United States to measure and report atmospheric pressure?", o:["Inches of mercury and millibars", "Knots and statute miles", "Degrees Celsius and degrees Fahrenheit", "Feet AGL and feet MSL"], a:0, r:"Inches of mercury and millibars. SG, Units of Measurement: \"In the U.S., two units are used to measure and report atmospheric pressure: inches of mercury (in-Hg) and millibars (mb). \"", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.174"},
    {q:"What is the standard (average) temperature lapse rate of the atmosphere in degrees Celsius per 1,000 feet?", o:["1.0", "2.0", "3.0", "3.57"], a:1, r:"2.0\u00b0 C per 1,000 ft. SG, Lapse Rates: \"The average or standard lapse rate is 2\u00b0 Celsius (3.57\u00b0 Fahrenheit) per 1,000 feet. \" Choice D is the same rate expressed in Fahrenheit \u2014 read the units.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.177"},
    {q:"Standard day conditions at sea level are:", o:["30.00 in-Hg and 59\u00b0 C", "1013.2 in-Hg and 15\u00b0 F", "29.92 in-Hg and 15\u00b0 C", "29.92 mb and 59\u00b0 C"], a:2, r:"29.92 in-Hg and 15\u00b0 C. SG, The Standard Atmosphere: \"standard day conditions at sea level, which are 29.92 in-Hg (1013.2 mb) and 15\u00b0 C (59\u00b0 F). \"", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.178"},
    {q:"Sea level pressure is:", o:["The atmospheric pressure measured directly at the airfield", "The pressure measured at 18,000 feet MSL", "Always 29.92 in-Hg", "The pressure that would be measured from the existing weather if the station were at mean sea level"], a:3, r:"SG, Station and Sea Level Pressure: \"Sea level pressure is the pressure that would be measured from the existing weather if the station were at mean sea level (MSL). \" Choice A defines station pressure.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.179"},
    {q:"Indicated altitude is:", o:["The altitude read directly from the altimeter", "The height of the aircraft above the terrain directly beneath it", "Pressure altitude corrected for nonstandard temperature", "The height above the standard datum plane"], a:0, r:"SG, Altitudes: \"Indicated altitude is the altitude read directly from the altimeter. \" B is absolute/AGL, C is density altitude, D is pressure altitude.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.181"},
    {q:"The height of an aircraft above the terrain directly beneath it is known as:", o:["MSL / true altitude", "Pressure altitude", "Indicated altitude", "AGL / absolute altitude"], a:3, r:"AGL / absolute altitude. SG, Altitudes: \"Absolute Altitude or AGL is the aircraft's height above the terrain directly beneath the aircraft. \"", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.180"},
    {q:"A change in pressure of 0.10 in-Hg will change the altimeter reading approximately:", o:["10 feet", "1,000 feet", "500 feet", "100 feet"], a:3, r:"100 feet. SG, Pressure: \"A change in pressure of 0.10 in-Hg will change the altimeter reading 100 feet. \" (The 1,000-ft figure corresponds to a full 1.00 in-Hg.)", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.182"},
    {q:"An air mass is defined as a large body of air that has essentially uniform:", o:["Pressure and stability in a vertical plane", "Density and lapse rate throughout its depth", "Wind speed and wind direction at all altitudes", "Temperature and moisture conditions in a horizontal plane"], a:3, r:"SG, Air Masses: \"An air mass is a large body of air that has essentially uniform temperature and moisture conditions in a horizontal plane. \"", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.184"},
    {q:"Air masses are named in part for their source region. Per the T.A.P.E. memory aid, the four source regions are:", o:["Tropical, Arctic, Polar, and Equatorial", "Temperate, Atlantic, Pacific, and European", "Tropical, Alpine, Polar, and Estuarine", "Terrestrial, Arctic, Pacific, and Equatorial"], a:0, r:"T.A.P.E.: Tropical, Arctic, Polar, Equatorial. Air masses are named according to their location (source region), temperature, and moisture. (Class gouge memory aid.)", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.184"},
    {q:"Relative humidity is:", o:["The temperature at which saturation occurs", "The difference between air temperature and dew point temperature", "The percent of saturation of the air", "The total weight of water vapor in a column of air"], a:2, r:"SG, Atmospheric Moisture: \"relative humidity (RH), which is the percent of saturation of the air. \" A is dew point; B is dew point depression/spread.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.295"},
    {q:"In the Northern Hemisphere, air circulates:", o:["Clockwise around low pressure and counterclockwise around high pressure", "Clockwise around both highs and lows", "Counterclockwise around both highs and lows", "Counterclockwise around low pressure and clockwise around high pressure"], a:3, r:"SG, Large Scale Wind Patterns: air circulates \"clockwise around high pressure, and counterclockwise around low pressure. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.185"},
    {q:"According to Buys Ballot's Law, if the wind is at your back in the Northern Hemisphere:", o:["Lower pressure will be to your left", "Lower pressure will be to your right", "Higher pressure will be directly ahead", "Higher pressure will be directly behind"], a:0, r:"Lower pressure to your left. SG, Buys Ballot's Law: \"if the wind is at your back, the area of lower pressure will be to your left. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.185"},
    {q:"Because of surface friction, surface winds blow across the isobars at an assumed angle of approximately:", o:["15\u00b0", "30\u00b0", "45\u00b0", "90\u00b0"], a:2, r:"45\u00b0 . SG, Surface Winds: \"This angle varies as a result of the type of terrain, but for our purposes, we will assume a 45\u00b0 angle. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.186"},
    {q:"The jet stream is best described as:", o:["A narrow band of strong winds of 50 knots or more near the tropopause", "A steady 20-knot wind found just above the surface", "A vertical current of air produced by surface heating", "A band of stratiform cloud found along a warm front"], a:0, r:"SG, The Jet Stream: \"The jet stream is a narrow band of strong winds of 50 knots or more that meanders vertically and horizontally around the hemisphere, \" reaching a maximum near the tropopause.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.187"},
    {q:"In coastal areas, local winds generally blow:", o:["From land to water both day and night", "From water to land both day and night", "From land to water during the day and from water to land at night", "From water to land during the day and from land to water at night"], a:3, r:"SG, Sea and Land Breezes: the daytime sea breeze blows from water to land; the nighttime land breeze blows from land to water.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.188 / 2.189"},
    {q:"Which cloud produces continuous rain, snow, or ice pellets with a base extending down to about 1,000 feet AGL?", o:["Cirrus", "Cumulonimbus", "Altocumulus", "Nimbostratus"], a:3, r:"Nimbostratus. SG, Special Clouds: \"A special cloud, nimbostratus, produces continuous rain, snow, or ice pellets. The cloud base will extend down to about 1,000 ft AGL. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.196"},
    {q:"Low clouds have bases ranging from just above the surface to:", o:["5,000 feet AGL", "20,000 feet AGL", "10,000 feet AGL", "6,500 feet AGL"], a:3, r:"6,500 feet AGL. SG, Types of Clouds: \"Low clouds, ranging from just above the surface to 6500 feet AGL. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.196"},
    {q:"The H.I.M.E.L.T. memory aid lists the hazards presented by thunderstorms. They are:", o:["Hail, Icing, Microbursts, Extreme turbulence, Lightning, and Tornadoes", "Haze, Ice fog, Mist, Eddies, Low ceilings, and Thermals", "Hail, Inversions, Mountain waves, Embedded cells, Low visibility, and Turbulence", "Humidity, Icing, Microbursts, Electrical failure, Lift loss, and Thrust loss"], a:0, r:"H.I.M.E.L.T.: Hail, Icing, Microbursts, Extreme turbulence, Lightning, Tornadoes \u2014 plus the gust front ahead of the storm. (Class gouge memory aid for the SG thunderstorm hazards.)", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.223"},
    {q:"Precipitation characterized by a sudden beginning and ending with abruptly changing intensity is called:", o:["Continuous", "Intermittent", "Showers", "Steady"], a:2, r:"Showers. SG, Characteristics of Precipitation: \"Showers \u2013 Characterized by a sudden beginning and ending, and abruptly changing intensity and/or sky conditions. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.195"},
    {q:"The four methods of lifting are:", o:["Convergence, frontal, orographic, and thermal", "Convergence, subsidence, orographic, and thermal", "Divergence, frontal, adiabatic, and convective", "Convection, conduction, radiation, and advection"], a:0, r:"SG, Methods of Lifting: \"The four methods of lifting are convergence, frontal, orographic, and thermal. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.199"},
    {q:"Lifted air that is warmer than the surrounding air will continue to rise when the lifting action is removed. This indicates:", o:["A stable condition", "A neutrally stable condition", "An unstable condition", "An isothermal condition"], a:2, r:"Unstable. SG, Atmospheric Stability: \"Lifted air that is warmer than the surrounding air continues to rise when the lifting action is removed because it is less dense \u2014 indicates an unstable condition. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.200"},
    {q:"Stratiform clouds are associated with which flight conditions?", o:["Rough flight and showery precipitation", "Smooth flight and continuous precipitation", "Rough flight and clear icing", "Smooth flight and good visibility"], a:1, r:"SG, Stability and Flight Conditions (Figure 2\u201119): stratiform clouds develop in stable air, which brings smooth flight and continuous (steady) precipitation. Note that stable air also brings poor visibility, so D is wrong.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.194"},
    {q:"A front is:", o:["A line of violent thunderstorms ahead of a low", "Any area of low ceilings and poor visibility", "A band of strong winds near the tropopause", "An area of discontinuity that forms between two contrasting air masses"], a:3, r:"SG, Frontal Systems: \"A front is an area of discontinuity that forms between two contrasting air masses when they are adjacent to each other. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.190"},
    {q:"Turbulence is defined as:", o:["Any irregular or disturbed flow in the atmosphere producing gusts and/or eddies", "A sudden change in wind speed or direction over a short distance", "A localized downward flow of air with velocities over 2,000 fpm", "A current of air moving contrary to the main current"], a:0, r:"SG, Turbulence: \"Turbulence is any irregular or disturbed flow in the atmosphere producing gusts and or eddies. \" B defines wind shear; C defines a microburst; D defines an eddy current.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.202"},
    {q:"Which one of the following is NOT one of the intensities used to report turbulence in a PIREP?", o:["Light", "Trace", "Severe", "Extreme"], a:1, r:"Trace. SG, Figure 3\u20111 / Turbulence Intensities: the four turbulence intensities are light, moderate, severe, extreme. \"Trace\" is an icing intensity, not a turbulence intensity \u2014 this is a classic swap.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.203"},
    {q:"The four types of turbulence, divided according to their causative factors, are:", o:["Thermal, mechanical, frontal, and large-scale wind shear", "Thermal, mechanical, orographic, and convergent", "Light, moderate, severe, and extreme", "Occasional, intermittent, continuous, and clear-air"], a:0, r:"SG, Turbulence: \"The different types of turbulence can be divided according to their causative factors: thermal, mechanical, frontal, and large-scale wind shear. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.205"},
    {q:"Mechanical turbulence can result from wind flowing over which of the following?", o:["Irregular terrain", "Buildings", "Mountains", "All of the above"], a:3, r:"All of the above. SG, Mechanical Turbulence: \"when the air near the surface of the Earth flows over obstructions, such as bluffs, hills, mountains, or buildings. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.207"},
    {q:"The four types of structural icing are:", o:["Clear, rime, mixed, and frost", "Clear, rime, induction, and compressor", "Light, moderate, severe, and trace", "Glaze, hoar, sleet, and graupel"], a:0, r:"SG, Aircraft Icing: \"The types of structural icing are clear, rime, mixed, and frost. \" Induction and compressor icing are engine icing, not structural.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.213"},
    {q:"What conditions are required for the formation of aircraft structural icing?", o:["Freezing temperatures and invisible moisture", "Super-cooled visible water droplets, with free air temperature and aircraft surface temperature below freezing", "Freezing temperatures and strong headwinds", "Freezing temperatures and relative humidity above 75 percent"], a:1, r:"SG, Icing Requirements: \"the atmosphere must have super-cooled visible water droplets. Second, the free air temperature\u2026 and the aircraft's surface temperature must be below freezing. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.214"},
    {q:"Clear icing normally occurs at temperatures between:", o:["0\u00b0 C and \u201310\u00b0 C", "\u201310\u00b0 C and \u201320\u00b0 C", "\u20138\u00b0 C and \u201315\u00b0 C", "\u201320\u00b0 C and \u201340\u00b0 C"], a:0, r:"0\u00b0 C to \u201310\u00b0 C. SG, Structural Icing Conditions: \"Clear icing normally occurs at temperatures between 0\u00b0 C and \u201310\u00b0 C. \" (\u201310 to \u201320 is rime; \u20138 to \u201315 is mixed.)", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.215"},
    {q:"An aerodynamic hazard associated with structural icing is that it:", o:["Reduces lift and increases stall speed", "Increases lift and decreases stall speed", "Decreases drag and decreases weight", "Has no effect until at least one inch has accumulated"], a:0, r:"SG, Effects and Hazards of Structural Icing: ice \"decreases lift, thrust, and range, and increases drag, weight, fuel consumption, and stall speed. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.216"},
    {q:"Engine failure due to icing in a jet aircraft is generally the result of:", o:["Carburetor icing", "Structural icing on the wings", "Induction icing", "Frost on the empennage"], a:2, r:"Induction icing. SG, Other Types of Aircraft Icing: induction icing forms in the air intake duct; the reduced pressure at the intake lowers the temperature enough for condensation/deposition. Carburetor icing (A) does not apply to a jet.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.217"},
    {q:"Fog is a visible aggregate of minute water droplets based at or within 50 feet of the surface that reduces prevailing visibility to less than:", o:["1 statute mile", "3 statute miles", "\u215d statute mile", "7 statute miles"], a:2, r:"\u215d statute mile. SG, Fog vs Stratus: fog \"is based at or within 50 feet of the surface, is greater than 20 feet in depth, and reduces the prevailing visibility to less than \u215d of a statute mile. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.219"},
    {q:"Which type of fog forms when warm, moist air moves over a colder surface?", o:["Radiation fog", "Advection fog", "Valley fog", "Steam fog"], a:1, r:"Advection fog. SG, Advection Fog: \"Advection fog occurs when warm, moist air moves over a cold surface and the air is cooled to the dew point. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.222"},
    {q:"The basic requirements for thunderstorm formation are:", o:["Moisture, stable air, a lifting action, and buildup through the freezing layer", "Moisture, unstable air, a lifting action, and buildup through the freezing layer", "Dry air, subsidence, and a temperature inversion", "High pressure, light winds, and clear skies"], a:1, r:"SG, Thunderstorm Development: \"The basic requirements for thunderstorm formation are moisture, unstable air, some type of lifting action, and building up through the freezing layer. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.223"},
    {q:"The FIRST indication of penetrating a microburst on takeoff is:", o:["A rapid increase in indicated airspeed caused by the sudden headwind", "An immediate tailwind with a strong sink rate", "A total loss of lift with no airspeed change", "A rapid drop in engine temperature"], a:0, r:"Entering the microburst's outflow ring, the aircraft first meets a massive headwind: IAS, angle of attack, and lift all suddenly increase. The deadly tailwind and sink come moments later \u2014 which is why the initial performance gain must not be trusted.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.224"},
    {q:"Prevailing visibility is:", o:["The distance on final approach at which the runway environment is in sight", "The greatest horizontal visibility equaled or exceeded throughout at least half the horizon circle", "The average forward horizontal distance measured from the cockpit in flight", "The horizontal distance a pilot will see looking down the runway from the approach end"], a:1, r:"SG, Visibility Definitions: \"Prevailing Visibility \u2013 The greatest horizontal visibility, measured in statute miles, equaled or exceeded throughout at least half the horizon circle. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.226"},
    {q:"Runway Visual Range (RVR) is:", o:["The distance on final approach at which the runway environment is in sight", "The greatest horizontal visibility equaled or exceeded throughout at least half the horizon circle", "The horizontal distance a pilot will see by looking down the runway from the approach end", "The vertical distance seen upward into an obscuration"], a:2, r:"SG, Visibility Definitions: \"Runway Visual Range (RVR) \u2013 The horizontal distance\u2026 a pilot will see by looking down the runway from the approach end. \" A is slant range visibility.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.226"},
    {q:"A sky coverage of 5/8 through 7/8 is reported as:", o:["FEW", "SCT", "BKN", "OVC"], a:2, r:"BKN. SG, Figure 4\u20113 / Figure 4\u201120: FEW = 1/8\u20132/8, SCT = 3/8\u20134/8, BKN = 5/8\u20137/8, OVC = 8/8.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.227"},
    {q:"A ceiling is the height AGL ascribed to:", o:["The lowest few or scattered layer", "The lowest broken or overcast layer, or the vertical visibility into a total obscuration", "The highest overcast layer reported", "The top of the lowest cloud layer"], a:1, r:"SG, Sky Coverage and Ceilings: \"A ceiling is the height above the ground (AGL) ascribed to the lowest broken or overcast layer; or the vertical visibility into an obscuring phenomenon (total obscuration). \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.227"},
    {q:"The information displayed on a Surface Analysis Chart is:", o:["Observed weather, representing past history", "A forecast valid for the next 24 hours", "A forecast valid for up to one week", "Winds and temperatures aloft only"], a:0, r:"SG, Surface Analysis: \"The information displayed on the Surface Analysis Chart is observed weather, meaning that the chart represents past history, and is not a forecast. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.18"},
    {q:"Prognostic (Prog) Charts provide:", o:["Only the current observed surface analysis", "The latest surface analysis along with multiple forecasts for up to a week", "Radar returns from the previous hour", "Satellite imagery in the infrared spectrum"], a:1, r:"SG, Prognostic Charts: to a week. \" \"These charts include the latest surface analysis along with multiple forecasts for up", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.20"},
    {q:"In a METAR, the group \"27004KT\" indicates:", o:["Wind from 270 degrees at 4 knots", "Wind toward 270 degrees at 4 knots", "Wind from 004 degrees at 27 knots", "Wind from 270 degrees at 40 knots"], a:0, r:"SG, Group 4: Wind: direction is given first in three digits (270\u00b0), speed in the following digits (04 kt), and wind is always reported as the direction it blows FROM.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.23"},
    {q:"In a METAR, the group \"A2997\" indicates:", o:["An altimeter setting of 2,997 millibars", "An altimeter setting of 29.97 inches of mercury", "A sea level pressure of 999.7 millibars", "A station pressure of 29.97 millibars"], a:1, r:"SG, Group 10: Altimeter Setting: \"The altimeter group always starts with the letter \u2018A' , and will be followed with a 4-digit group using the tens, units, tenths, and hundredths of inches of mercury. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.23"},
    {q:"A Terminal Aerodrome Forecast (TAF) provides:", o:["The latest observed weather at a station", "Forecast surface weather conditions for an airfield", "A depiction of pressure centers and fronts", "Winds and temperatures aloft"], a:1, r:"SG, The Terminal Aerodrome Forecast (TAF): the METAR provides existing weather; the TAF provides \"the surface forecasted weather conditions. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.24"},
    {q:"On ground-based weather radar, the strongest return signals are yielded by:", o:["Snow", "Small water droplets", "Large drops of water and hail", "Clear air between cells"], a:2, r:"SG, RADAR information: \"The large drops of water and hail, if present, within thunderstorms yield the strongest return signals\u2026 snow produces the faintest echo. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.28"},
    {q:"The National Weather Service defines a severe thunderstorm as one producing hail at least ___ in diameter, and/or wind gusts of ___ or greater, and/or a tornado.", o:["1 inch; 58 mph", "2 inches; 75 mph", "\u00bd inch; 35 mph", "\u00be inch; 50 mph"], a:0, r:"1 inch hail; 58 mph winds. SG, Severe Weather Watch Bulletins: \"A severe thunderstorm refers to a thunderstorm producing hail that is at least 1 inch in diameter or larger, and/or wind gusts of 58 mph or greater, and/or a tornado. \" Choice B is the significant severe threshold.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.30"},
  ]
},
{
  name: "Test 2 \u2014 Medium",
  desc: "Applied relationships, decode drills, and plausible near-miss distractors.",
  questions: [
    {q:"Which one of the following correctly states the height of the troposphere?", o:["28,000 ft MSL over the equator, 55,000 ft MSL over the poles, 36,000 ft MSL average over the U.S.", "53,000 ft MSL over the equator, 36,000 ft MSL over the poles, 28,000 ft MSL average over the U.S.", "36,000 ft MSL over the equator, 18,000 ft MSL over the poles, 53,000 ft MSL average over the U.S.", "55,000 ft MSL over the equator, 28,000 ft MSL over the poles, 36,000 ft MSL average over the U.S."], a:3, r:"SG, Troposphere: \"It varies in height from an average 55,000 feet MSL over the equator to 28,000 feet MSL over the poles. The average height of the troposphere over the United States is 36,000 feet MSL. \" Choice A is the same numbers with the equator and poles reversed \u2014 the classic trap.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.172"},
    {q:"Approximately 50% of the atmosphere by weight lies below ______, and 90% lies within ______.", o:["18,000 feet; 53,000 feet", "18,000 feet; 36,000 feet", "36,000 feet; 55,000 feet", "28,000 feet; 55,000 feet"], a:0, r:"18,000 ft / 53,000 ft. SG, Troposphere: \"roughly 50% of it, by weight, lies below 18,000 feet and 90% within 53,000 feet. \" Note 53,000 ft is not the same as the 55,000 ft equatorial troposphere height in Q1.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.172"},
    {q:"Which statement about the tropopause is correct?", o:["Temperature decreases rapidly with altitude, and the weakest winds are found there", "Temperature increases with altitude, and it is where nearly all weather occurs", "Temperature is constant with altitude change, and the strongest winds occur just below it", "Temperature is constant with altitude change, and the strongest winds occur just above it"], a:2, r:"SG, Troposphere / tropopause: \"The temperature in this layer is constant with altitude change\u2026 the strongest winds, those of the jet stream, occur just below the tropopause. \" D is correct on temperature but wrong on where the jet stream sits.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.172"},
    {q:"Isobars that are closely spaced on a surface analysis chart indicate:", o:["A shallow pressure gradient and slower wind speeds", "A steep pressure gradient and slower wind speeds", "A shallow pressure gradient and faster wind speeds", "A steep pressure gradient and faster wind speeds"], a:3, r:"SG, Pressure Gradients: \"The PGF is steep, or strong, when isobars are close together\u2026 the steeper the gradient, the stronger the winds. \"", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.175"},
    {q:"In the lower atmosphere, a 1,000-foot increase in altitude results in a pressure decrease of approximately:", o:["1 in-Hg (34 mb)", "0.10 in-Hg (3.4 mb)", "2 in-Hg (68 mb)", "1 mb (0.03 in-Hg)"], a:0, r:"1 in-Hg (34 mb). SG, The Standard Atmosphere: \"a 1,000 foot increase in altitude will result in a pressure decrease of approximately 1 in-Hg (34 mb). \" Choice B is the 0.10 in-Hg = 100 ft relationship.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.178"},
    {q:"A station is located at 2,500 feet MSL and is reporting a sea level pressure of 1045.0 mb. Assuming the standard pressure lapse rate, what is the station pressure?", o:["960.0 mb", "1045.0 mb", "1047.0 mb", "1130.0 mb"], a:0, r:"960.0 mb. Field elevation is 2,500 ft; at 34 mb per 1,000 ft that is a correction of 85 mb. Sea level pressure = station pressure + correction, so station pressure = 1045.0 \u2013 85 = 960.0 mb. Pressure always decreases with altitude, so the station's own pressure must be lower than the sea level value \u2014 which eliminates C and D immediately. (Note: one of the legacy practice tests keys this question as 1130.0 mb. That answer is inconsistent with the SG and with the physics; 960 is correct.)", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.179"},
    {q:"Using the standard lapse rate, a pilot at 10,000 feet MSL with an outside air temperature of \u20138\u00b0 C should do what to reach an altitude where the temperature is +4\u00b0 C?", o:["Descend to approximately 2,000 feet MSL", "Climb to find an inversion", "Descend to approximately 6,000 feet MSL", "Descend to approximately 4,000 feet MSL"], a:3, r:"Descend to approximately 4,000 ft MSL. A change from \u20138\u00b0 C to +4\u00b0 C is 12\u00b0 C. At 2\u00b0 C per 1,000 ft that is 6,000 feet of descent: 10,000 \u2013 6,000 = 4,000 ft MSL. Choice C is the amount of the descent, not the resulting altitude.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.177"},
    {q:"Which type of altitude is required in the United States above 18,000 feet MSL, and what altimeter setting corresponds to it?", o:["True altitude; local altimeter setting", "Absolute altitude; local altimeter setting", "Pressure altitude; 29.92 in-Hg", "Density altitude; 29.92 in-Hg"], a:2, r:"SG, Altitudes: \"Federal Aviation Rules (FAR) require that all aircraft operating above 18,000 feet MSL set 29.92 in to the altimeter. \" That is by definition a pressure altitude.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.180"},
    {q:"An aircraft at Sherman Field (elevation 28 ft MSL) shows an indicated altitude of 70 feet with the local altimeter setting in the Kollsman window. This aircraft:", o:["Has an instrument error of +42 feet and is legal for instrument flight", "Has an instrument error of +98 feet and is not legal for instrument flight", "Has an instrument error of \u201342 feet and is not legal for instrument flight", "Has no instrument error; the difference is caused by nonstandard temperature"], a:0, r:"SG, Altitudes: instrument error is the difference between field elevation and indicated altitude \u2014 70 \u2013 28 = +42 ft. \"If the instrument error is in excess of 75 ft, the aircraft is considered unsafe for instrument flight, \" so +42 ft is within limits.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.181"},
    {q:"An increase in density altitude will:", o:["Shorten takeoff distance and increase rate of climb", "Lengthen takeoff distance and increase rate of climb", "Shorten takeoff distance and reduce rate of climb", "Lengthen takeoff distance and reduce rate of climb"], a:3, r:"SG, Density altitude: rate of climb. \" \"higher density altitudes result in longer takeoff and landing distances and a reduced", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.180"},
    {q:"The altimeter setting at Randolph AFB is 29.85 in-Hg; at Vance AFB it is 30.15 in-Hg. A pilot sets the altimeter correctly at Randolph and flies to Vance at an indicated altitude of 5,000 feet without resetting. Assuming a standard lapse rate, the true (MSL) altitude over Vance is:", o:["4,700 feet", "5,000 feet", "5,030 feet", "5,300 feet"], a:3, r:"5,300 feet. This is a low-to-high pressure change (\"Low to High, Plenty of Sky\"), so the aircraft is higher than indicated. Altitude error = (30.15 \u2013 29.85) \u00d7 1,000 ft/in-Hg = 300 ft. MSL = assigned altitude + altitude error = 5,000 + 300 = 5,300 ft. Choice A applies the error in the wrong direction; choice C uses 0.10 in-Hg = 10 ft.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.182"},
    {q:"Cold air masses are considered ______ because the air is ______ than the surface it moves over.", o:["stable; colder", "unstable; warmer", "stable; warmer", "unstable; colder"], a:3, r:"unstable; colder. SG, Air Masses: \"cold air masses are inherently unstable. This is because cold air masses typically come from the north and consist of colder air moving over warmer surfaces. \" The air is colder than the surface beneath it, so it is heated from below \u2014 the classic recipe for instability. (Warm air masses are the reverse: warmer than the surface, therefore stable.) This matches the SG's own review question 5\u20111\u20113 #19.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.184"},
    {q:"Once the dew point spread reaches approximately 2\u00b0 C (4\u00b0 F), the relative humidity will be about ______ and water vapor will begin to condense into fog or clouds.", o:["100%", "50%", "75%", "90%"], a:3, r:"90%. SG, Atmospheric Moisture: \"Once the dew point spread reaches about 2\u00b0 C / 4\u00b0 F, the RH will be 90%, and the water vapor will begin to condense into fog or clouds. \" 100% RH corresponds to a spread of zero.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.197 / 2.198"},
    {q:"Gradient winds:", o:["Flow parallel to the isobars and are found above 2,000 feet AGL", "Flow parallel to the isobars and are found below 2,000 feet AGL", "Flow across the isobars at 45\u00b0 and are found above 2,000 feet AGL", "Flow perpendicular to the isobars at all altitudes"], a:0, r:"SG, Gradient Winds: gradient winds \"flow parallel to the isobars\u2026 gradient winds are found above 2,000 feet AGL. \" Below 2,000 ft AGL friction produces the 45\u00b0 cross-isobar surface wind.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.185"},
    {q:"Which statement correctly pairs a force with its role in producing wind?", o:["The Coriolis force is the initiating force for all winds", "Friction is the initiating force for all winds", "The pressure gradient force is the initiating force for all winds, and the Coriolis force deflects the air to the right in the Northern Hemisphere", "The pressure gradient force deflects the air to the right, and the Coriolis force initiates all winds"], a:2, r:"SG, Pressure Gradients / Gradient Winds: \"The PGF is the initiating force for all winds, \" and \"The Coriolis force, created by the Earth's rotation, diverts the air to the right. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.185"},
    {q:"Jet stream winds average about ______ knots, but may reach speeds in excess of ______ knots.", o:["50 to 100; 200", "200 to 250; 350", "150 to 200; 300", "100 to 150; 250"], a:3, r:"100 to 150 kt average, in excess of 250 kt. SG, The Jet Stream: \"These winds average about 100\u2011150 knots but may reach speeds in excess of 250 knots. \" (50 kt is the minimum threshold that defines a jet stream, not the average.)", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.187"},
    {q:"Jet streams are most frequently found in segments of:", o:["100 to 200 miles in length, 10 to 50 miles wide, 2,000 to 10,000 feet in height", "1,000 to 3,000 miles in length, 10 to 40 miles wide, 30,000 to 40,000 feet in height", "500 to 1,000 miles in length, 50 to 100 miles wide, 5,000 to 15,000 feet in height", "1,000 to 3,000 miles in length, 100 to 400 miles wide, 3,000 to 7,000 feet in height"], a:3, r:"SG, The Jet Stream: \"found in segments from 1,000 to 3,000 miles in length, 100 to 400 miles in depth/width, and 3,000 to 7,000 feet in height. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.187"},
    {q:"Per the frontal model, a warm front moves at an average speed of ____ toward the ____, with a 90\u00b0 wind shift from ____ to ____.", o:["15 knots; northeast; southeast to southwest", "20 knots; southeast; southwest to northwest", "15 knots; southwest; northwest to southeast", "25 knots; northeast; south to north"], a:0, r:"Frontal model: warm fronts average 15 knots toward the NE with a 90\u00b0 shift SE to SW. (Cold fronts: 20 knots toward the SE, shift SW to NW. Occluded: speed depends, toward the NE, 180\u00b0 shift SE to NW.)", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"Middle clouds have bases between ______ and carry the prefix ______.", o:["6,500 and 20,000 ft AGL; alto-", "5,000 and 18,500 ft AGL; alto-", "6,500 and 20,000 ft AGL; cirro-", "6,500 and 20,000 ft MSL; nimbo-"], a:0, r:"SG, Middle Clouds: \"cloud bases form between 6,500 and 20,000 feet AGL. The names of the middle clouds will contain the prefix alto- . \" Cloud heights in this course are always AGL, which eliminates D; cirro- is the high cloud prefix.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.196"},
    {q:"A pilot approaching an active warm front from the east would encounter the cloud sequence:", o:["Stratus, nimbostratus, altostratus, cirrostratus, cirrus", "Altocumulus, cumulonimbus, fair weather cumulus", "Cumulus, towering cumulus, cumulonimbus, anvil cirrus", "Cirrus, cirrostratus, altostratus, nimbostratus, stratus"], a:3, r:"SG, Recognizing Warm Fronts During Flight: \"If one were to approach the front from the east, the sequence of clouds would be cirrus, cirrostratus, altostratus, nimbostratus, and stratus, rain and fog. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"On average, a cold front moves toward the ______ at ______ knots, with a wind shift from ______.", o:["NE; 15; SE to SW", "NE; 20; SE to NW", "SE; 15; SW to NW", "SE; 20; SW to NW"], a:3, r:"SE at 20 kt, wind shift SW to NW. SG, Cold Fronts: \"Cold fronts move toward the SE at 20 kts, on average, and the wind shift is from the SW to the NW. \" Choice A is the warm front (NE at 15 kt, SE to SW).", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"A stationary front is characterized by:", o:["A 90\u00b0 wind shift from SE to SW and movement toward the NE at 15 knots", "A 90\u00b0 wind shift from SW to NW and rapidly clearing postfrontal skies", "A 180\u00b0 wind shift and movement toward the NE at the speed of the front remaining on the ground", "A 180\u00b0 wind shift and little or no movement, with weather similar to but less intense than a warm front"], a:3, r:"SG, Stationary Fronts: \"a stationary front has a 180\u00b0 wind shift\" ; \"The weather conditions occurring with a stationary front are similar to those found with the warm front, but are usually less intense. \" Choice C describes an occlusion.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"Occluded fronts are depicted on surface analysis charts in ______ and generally move toward the ______.", o:["purple; NE", "purple; SE", "red and blue alternating; NE", "blue; SE"], a:0, r:"Purple; NE. SG, Occluded Fronts: \"Occlusions are shown on surface charts with both cold and warm frontal symbols pointing in the same direction, but colored purple. Both types of occlusions tend to be aligned from NW to SE, and hence move toward the NE. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"A squall line is a line of violent thunderstorms that typically develops:", o:["5 to 20 miles ahead of a cold front and perpendicular to it", "50 to 300 miles ahead of a cold front and roughly parallel to it", "500 to 700 miles ahead of a warm front and roughly parallel to it", "Directly along the surface position of a warm front"], a:1, r:"SG, Squall Lines: \"They develop 50 to 300 miles ahead of the cold front and roughly parallel to it. \" Choice A is the gust front distance (5\u201320 miles); choice C is the extent of the warm front cloud shield.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.193"},
    {q:"Which set of properties is used to locate and classify fronts?", o:["Temperature, moisture (dew point), winds, and pressure", "Temperature, altitude, density, and stability", "Ceiling, visibility, precipitation, and icing", "Isotachs, isotherms, isogonic lines, and contour lines"], a:0, r:"SG, Frontal Discontinuities: \"Differences in the various properties of adjacent air masses\u2013in particular, their temperature, moisture (indicated by the dew point), winds, and pressure\u2013are used to locate and classify fronts. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.191"},
    {q:"Which grouping correctly lists \"signs in the sky\" that indicate a stable atmosphere?", o:["Thunderstorms, showers, towering clouds, dust devils", "Temperature inversions, low fog and stratus, rising air temperature while climbing", "Rapidly decreasing air temperature while climbing, cumuliform clouds, gusty winds", "Good visibility, showery precipitation, clear icing"], a:1, r:"SG, Atmospheric Stability: \"Here are some additional \u2018signs in the sky' that indicate stable air: temperature inversions, low fog and stratus, and rising air temperature while climbing. \" Choices A and C list the unstable indicators; D mixes unstable indicators in.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.194 / 2.200"},
    {q:"A pilot encountering turbulence between one-third and two-thirds of the time would report the turbulence as:", o:["Occasional", "Continuous", "Intermittent", "Moderate"], a:2, r:"Intermittent. SG, Figure 3\u20111 / Turbulence Intensities and Reporting: occasional = less than \u2153 of the time; intermittent = \u2153 to \u2154; continuous = more than \u2154. Note that \"moderate\" (D) is an intensity, not a time descriptor.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.204"},
    {q:"Clear air turbulence (CAT) normally occurs:", o:["Below 15,000 feet MSL and is caused by surface heating", "Above 15,000 feet MSL and is caused by strong wind shears in the jet stream", "Above 15,000 feet MSL and is caused exclusively by mountain waves", "Only within cumulonimbus clouds"], a:1, r:"SG, Clear Air Turbulence: \"CAT normally occurs outside of clouds and usually occurs at altitudes above 15,000 feet MSL, due to strong wind shears in the jet stream. \" C is too absolute \u2014 CAT can occur in all four turbulence categories.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.211"},
    {q:"The strength of thermal (convective) turbulence depends in part on the extent to which the Earth's surface has been heated, which in turn depends on the nature of the surface. Which surface would produce the strongest convective currents on a clear, sunny day?", o:["A plowed field", "A grassy field", "A forested area", "A lake"], a:0, r:"Plowed field. SG, Thermal Turbulence: \"dry, barren surfaces such as sandy or rocky wasteland and plowed fields absorb heat more readily than surfaces covered with grass or other vegetation\u2026 Thus, barren surfaces generally cause stronger convective currents. \" Water surfaces heat most slowly.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.206"},
    {q:"The strength and vertical extent of mechanical turbulence depends on wind speed, terrain roughness, and air stability. Which of these appears to be the most important factor?", o:["Wind speed", "Terrain roughness", "Stability of the air", "Air temperature"], a:2, r:"Stability of the air. SG, Mechanical Turbulence: \"Stability seems to be the most important factor in determining the strength and vertical extent of the mechanical turbulence. \" Wind speed and terrain roughness are contributing factors, but the SG singles out stability.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.207"},
    {q:"Dangerous, gusty winds that occur 5 to 20 miles ahead of an approaching thunderstorm are called:", o:["A gust front", "A squall line", "Virga", "A microburst"], a:0, r:"The gust front is the arc of dangerous, gusty downdraft outflow 5-20 miles ahead of the storm \u2014 the reason you never take off or land with a thunderstorm approaching. It is NOT the same thing as a squall line (a line of storms ahead of a cold front).", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.224"},
    {q:"When taking off or landing in near-calm surface winds under clear skies within a few hours of sunrise, a pilot should expect a shear zone at an inversion if the wind at 2,000 to 4,000 feet AGL is:", o:["10 knots or more", "15 knots or more", "25 knots or more", "50 knots or more"], a:2, r:"25 knots or more. SG, Temperature Inversions: \"If the wind at 2000 to 4000 feet AGL is 25 knots or more, expect a shear zone at the inversion. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.209"},
    {q:"Which one of the following is NOT a recommended procedure for flying through turbulence?", o:["Establish and maintain thrust settings consistent with turbulent air penetration airspeed", "Control pitch and bank by reference to the attitude gyro indicator", "Chase the altimeter to hold assigned altitude precisely", "Use moderate control inputs to reestablish the desired attitude"], a:2, r:"SG, Flight Techniques for Turbulence: \"Allow altitude to vary. Sacrifice altitude to maintain desired attitude. Don't chase the altimeter. \" The other three are verbatim recommendations.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.212"},
    {q:"Rime icing is best described as:", o:["Milky white, forming from small droplets in stable clouds at \u201310\u00b0 to \u201320\u00b0 C", "Glossy and transparent, forming from large droplets in unstable clouds at 0\u00b0 to \u201310\u00b0 C", "Lumpy but hard and dense, forming at \u20138\u00b0 to \u201315\u00b0 C", "A thin layer of crystalline ice formed by deposition on a parked aircraft"], a:0, r:"SG, Structural Icing Conditions: \"Rime icing is milky white in appearance and is most likely to occur at temperatures of \u201310 to \u201320\u00b0 C\u2026 Rime ice occurs in stable conditions\u2013clouds where the water droplets are small. \" B describes clear ice, C describes mixed, D describes frost.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.213 / 2.215"},
    {q:"The most frequently encountered type of aircraft icing is:", o:["Clear ice", "Rime ice", "Mixed ice", "Frost"], a:2, r:"Mixed ice. SG, Structural Icing Conditions: \"The most frequent type of icing encountered is usually a form of mixed icing. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.213"},
    {q:"A pilot encounters clear icing in freezing precipitation below the clouds of a warm front. The best course of action is generally to:", o:["Continue on the current flight path until clear of the precipitation", "Descend, regardless of the minimum en route altitude", "Climb to warmer temperatures across the frontal boundary", "Fly parallel to the front until out of the icing"], a:2, r:"SG, Minimizing or Avoiding Icing Hazards: \"if encountering clear icing in the freezing precipitation below the clouds of a warm front, the aircraft is most likely in the cold air ahead of the warm front. In this case, the best alternative may be to climb to warmer temperatures, across the frontal boundary, as the freezing precipitation may extend all the way to ground level. \" Choice D is explicitly prohibited (\"Do not fly parallel to a front while encountering icing conditions\").", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.218"},
    {q:"Which set correctly lists the three conditions required for fog to form?", o:["Condensation nuclei, a high water content (low dew point spread), and light surface winds", "Condensation nuclei, a large dew point spread, and calm winds", "High water content, freezing temperatures, and clear skies", "Condensation nuclei, unstable air, and a lifting action"], a:0, r:"SG, Fog Formation: \"For fog to form, three conditions must be satisfied: (1) condensation nuclei must be present in the air, (2) the air must have a high water content (a low dew point spread), (3) and light surface winds must be present. \" Choice D lists thunderstorm requirements.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.221"},
    {q:"With respect to radiation fog, winds of 5 to 10 knots will usually:", o:["Result in shallow fog", "Dissipate the fog and cause low stratus to form", "Cause dense fog", "Have no effect on fog formation"], a:2, r:"Cause dense fog. SG, Radiation Fog: \"Winds less than 5 knots usually results in shallow fog. Winds of 5 to 10 knots will usually cause dense fog. Winds of greater than 10 knots will usually dissipate the fog. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.222"},
    {q:"When it is necessary to fly over the top of a thunderstorm, the recommended altitude separation from the cloud tops is:", o:["1,000 feet above the tops", "100 feet for every 10 knots of wind speed at the altitude of the tops", "500 feet for every 10 knots of wind speed at the altitude of the tops", "1,000 feet for every 10 knots of wind speed at the altitude of the tops"], a:3, r:"SG, Over the Top: \"allow a margin of safety by choosing an altitude separation from the top of the thunderstorm of 1000 feet for every 10 knots of wind speed at the altitude of the tops. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.225"},
    {q:"Flight visibility is:", o:["The greatest horizontal visibility equaled or exceeded throughout at least half the horizon circle", "The average forward horizontal distance, measured from the cockpit of an aircraft in flight, at which a pilot can see and identify prominent unlighted objects by day and prominent lighted objects at night", "The distance on final approach at which the runway environment is in sight", "The distance seen down the runway from the approach end"], a:1, r:"SG, Visibility Definitions: \"Flight Visibility \u2013 The average forward horizontal distance, measured in statute miles from the cockpit of an aircraft in flight, at which a pilot can see and identify prominent unlighted objects by day and prominent lighted objects at night. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.226"},
    {q:"A report of \"SCT000\" in a METAR indicates:", o:["A ceiling of zero feet", "A partial obscuration, which does not constitute a ceiling", "A total obscuration with vertical visibility of zero", "Scattered clouds with bases at the standard datum plane"], a:1, r:"SG, Sky Coverage and Ceilings and Group 8: Sky Condition: a surface-based obscuration reported as FEW000/SCT000/BKN000 is a partial obscuration, and \"partial obscurations are not considered ceilings. \" A total obscuration would be coded VV.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.227"},
    {q:"Pilots are required to submit a PIREP in which of the following situations?", o:["Only when specifically requested by ATC", "When weather on an IFR approach differs from the latest observation, when wind shear is encountered on departure or arrival, when requested by ATC, when unusual or unforeseen weather is encountered, and when a missed approach is executed", "Only after landing, and only if the weather was worse than forecast", "Whenever any cloud layer is observed at or below 10,000 feet"], a:1, r:"SG, Pilot Weather Reports: the five required reporting conditions are listed verbatim.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.228"},
    {q:"A non-convective U.S. SIGMET is issued for which of the following?", o:["Moderate turbulence, moderate icing, and IFR conditions", "Severe icing, severe or extreme turbulence, dust/sandstorms lowering visibility below 3 miles, and volcanic ash", "Thunderstorms only", "Sustained surface winds greater than 30 knots and low-level wind shear"], a:1, r:"SG, SIGMETs: US (non-convective) SIGMETs are issued for \"Severe Icing; Severe or Extreme Turbulence; Dust Storms and/or Sand Storms lowering visibility to less than 3 miles; Volcanic Ash. \" Choice A lists AIRMET criteria; choice D lists specific G\u2011AIRMET (TANGO) items.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.229"},
    {q:"On a station model, the sea level pressure is depicted as \"985. \" Decoded in millibars, this is:", o:["985.0 mb", "998.5 mb", "1098.5 mb", "1985 mb"], a:1, r:"998.5 mb. SG, Station Model Plots: for millibars the hundreds/thousands digit and the decimal point are dropped. \"Pressures beginning with large numbers (such as a 9) really start with a hidden \u20189' , \" so 985 \u2192 998.5 mb. A value beginning with 1 or 0 would take a hidden \"10. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.21"},
    {q:"A METAR reports \"1/2SM FG VV004. \" This indicates:", o:["A cloud ceiling of 400 feet AGL with fog beneath it", "An indefinite ceiling with a vertical visibility of 400 feet into the fog", "A partial obscuration with 4/8 sky coverage", "Clouds at 4,000 feet AGL and fog at the surface"], a:1, r:"SG, Sky Coverage and Ceilings / Group 8: \"When the sky is totally obscured by a surface-based obscuration the only group in the sky condition section will be a 5-digit group, the first 2 digits VV\u2026 and the last 3 digits the height of the vertical visibility into the indefinite ceiling. \" VV004 = 400 ft. This is not the same as a 400-ft cloud ceiling, because the obscuration also degrades slant range visibility.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.23"},
    {q:"RVR is reported whenever:", o:["Prevailing visibility is 3 statute miles or less", "Prevailing visibility is 1 statute mile or less, and/or the RVR for the designated instrument runway is 6,000 feet or less", "The ceiling is at or below 1,000 feet", "Any obscuring phenomenon is present"], a:1, r:"SG, Group 6: Runway Visual Range: \"RVR is reported whenever the prevailing visibility is 1 statute mile or less and/or the RVR for the designated instrument runway is 6000 feet or less. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.23"},
    {q:"In a U.S. military TAF, a visibility code of \"9999\" indicates:", o:["Visibility of 9,999 meters exactly", "Visibility of 7 miles or greater (unlimited)", "Visibility is not forecast", "Visibility of 9,999 feet"], a:1, r:"SG, Visibility, Weather, and Obstructions to Vision: \"A visibility code of \u20189999' indicates 7 miles visibility or greater is forecast, i.e. unlimited visibility. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.25"},
    {q:"Which statement correctly distinguishes the TAF change groups?", o:["FM indicates a rapid, permanent change; BECMG indicates a slow, permanent change; TEMPO indicates a temporary, non-permanent change", "FM indicates a slow change; BECMG indicates a rapid change; TEMPO indicates a permanent change", "All three indicate permanent changes and differ only in duration", "TEMPO supersedes all previously forecast conditions for the remainder of the TAF"], a:0, r:"SG, Change Group Terminology: \"FM is used when the change is expected to be quick, and BECMG is used when the change is expected to occur over a longer period of time\u2026 TEMPO (Temporary) means just that: a temporary or non-permanent change. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.25"},
    {q:"On a Winds-Aloft Forecast, the code \"9900\" indicates:", o:["Winds from 099\u00b0 at 00 knots", "Winds light and variable", "Winds from 990\u00b0 \u2014 a coding error", "Winds from 090\u00b0 at 100 knots"], a:1, r:"Light and variable. SG, Special Circumstances 1: \"When forecast wind speeds are less than 5 knots, direction is difficult to determine, and the winds are called \u2018light and variable, ' and the code \u20189900' will be listed. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.27"},
    {q:"For flight planning purposes, an aviator must consider the worst weather conditions falling within which window?", o:["From 1 hour prior to ETA up to but not including 1 hour after ETA", "From 30 minutes prior to ETA up to 30 minutes after ETA", "From ETA to the end of the TAF period", "The entire 24-hour TAF period"], a:0, r:"SG, Using TAFs For Flight Planning: \"an aviator must consider the worst weather conditions that fall within the period of 1 hour prior to the planned estimated time of arrival (ETA) up to but not including 1 hour after ETA, for a total of a 2-hour window. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.25 / 4.32"},
  ]
},
{
  name: "Test 3 \u2014 Hard",
  desc: "Near-miss distractors, NOT-style stems, and applied scenarios. Read every option.",
  questions: [
    {q:"Which one of the following statements is NOT correct?", o:["Pressure always decreases with altitude", "In the lower atmosphere, pressure decreases more rapidly with altitude than it does at higher altitudes", "Temperature always decreases with altitude within the troposphere", "Close to the surface, the measured lapse rate may show an increase, a decrease, or no change in temperature"], a:2, r:"(the incorrect statement). SG, Troposphere: \"Within the troposphere, the temperature normally decreases with increasing altitude\" \u2014 and Lapse Rates: \"close to the surface of the earth the lapse rate may indicate an increase, decrease, or a constant temperature. \" Inversions and isothermal layers exist inside the troposphere, so \"always\" makes B false. A, C, and D are all verbatim SG statements. Pressure, unlike temperature, always decreases with altitude \u2014 that is the asymmetry this question tests.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.173 / 2.176"},
    {q:"Which one of the following correctly lists standard day sea level pressure, temperature, pressure lapse rate, and temperature lapse rate?", o:["29.92 in-Hg, 15\u00b0 C, 1 in-Hg/1,000 ft, 2\u00b0 C/1,000 ft", "29.92 in-Hg, 59\u00b0 C, 1 in-Hg/1,000 ft, 2\u00b0 C/1,000 ft", "29.92 in-Hg, 15\u00b0 C, 1 in-Hg/1,000 ft, 3.57\u00b0 C/1,000 ft", "30.00 in-Hg, 15\u00b0 C, 0.10 in-Hg/1,000 ft, 2\u00b0 C/1,000 ft"], a:0, r:"SG, The Standard Atmosphere: 29.92 in-Hg (1013.2 mb), 15\u00b0 C (59\u00b0 F), 1 in-Hg (34 mb) per 1,000 ft, 2\u00b0 C (3.57\u00b0 F) per 1,000 ft. B swaps 15\u00b0 C for 59 \u2014 but attaches the C unit to the Fahrenheit number. C uses the Fahrenheit lapse rate with a Celsius label. Each wrong answer contains exactly one unit or digit error.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.178"},
    {q:"For every ______ that the temperature varies from the standard atmosphere, the altimeter will be in error by ______.", o:["4\u00b0 C; 11%", "2\u00b0 C; 4%", "11\u00b0 C; 4%", "11\u00b0 F; 4%"], a:2, r:"11\u00b0 C; 4%. SG, Temperature: \"For every 11 \u00b0C that the temperature varies from the standard, the altimeter will be in error by 4%. \" Choice A inverts the two numbers; choice D changes the unit to Fahrenheit.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.183"},
    {q:"An aircraft departs an area of high pressure and warm air and flies, without resetting the altimeter, into an area of both lower pressure and colder air. Relative to the indicated altitude, the aircraft will be:", o:["Higher than indicated, because both effects raise true altitude", "At the indicated altitude, because the two effects cancel", "Higher than indicated, because the pressure effect dominates the temperature effect", "Lower than indicated, because both effects lower true altitude"], a:3, r:"SG, Pressure and Temperature: \"High to Low, Look Out Below = The aircraft is actually at a lower altitude than indicated, \" and \"As you fly from warm to cold air, an altimeter will read too high\u2013the aircraft is lower than the altimeter indicates. \" Both errors act in the same direction here, so they compound rather than cancel. This is the pairing that kills aircraft in cold weather over mountains.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.182 / 2.183"},
    {q:"A pilot departs NAS Pensacola (field elevation 30 ft MSL, altimeter setting 30.00 in-Hg) and flies to NAS Memphis (field elevation 300 ft MSL, altimeter setting 30.20 in-Hg) at an assigned indicated altitude of 1,000 feet, without resetting the altimeter. Over Memphis, the aircraft's AGL/absolute altitude is approximately:", o:["700 feet AGL", "1,000 feet AGL", "900 feet AGL", "1,200 feet AGL"], a:2, r:"900 feet AGL. Two steps. (1) Pressure change is low to high (30.00 \u2192 30.20), so \"Low to High, Plenty of Sky\" : altitude error = 0.20 \u00d7 1,000 = 200 ft, and MSL = assigned altitude + error = 1,000 + 200 = 1,200 ft MSL. (2) AGL = MSL \u2013 field elevation = 1,200 \u2013 300 = 900 ft AGL. Choice D (1,200 ft) is the correct MSL answer to a question that asked for AGL \u2014 read the altitude reference being requested.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.182"},
    {q:"A pilot sets the altimeter correctly at Randolph AFB (29.85 in-Hg) and lands at Vance AFB (elevation 1,307 ft MSL, altimeter setting 30.15 in-Hg) without ever resetting the altimeter. On deck at Vance, the altimeter will indicate approximately:", o:["0 feet", "1,607 feet", "1,307 feet", "1,007 feet"], a:3, r:"1,007 feet. SG, Pressure: for a low-to-high change, \"Indicated Altitude on Deck = Field Elevation (\u2013) Altitude Error. \" Altitude error = (30.15 \u2013 29.85) \u00d7 1,000 = 300 ft, so 1,307 \u2013 300 = 1,007 ft. Note that in flight the aircraft was higher than indicated (Q11 of Test 2), yet on deck the altimeter reads lower than field elevation \u2014 same rule, applied to the correct formula.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.182"},
    {q:"Which one of the following statements about density altitude is correct?", o:["It is a height reference measured above the standard datum plane", "It is true altitude corrected for instrument error and nonstandard pressure", "It is pressure altitude corrected for nonstandard temperature and serves as an index to aircraft performance rather than a height reference", "It is always higher than the published field elevation"], a:2, r:"SG, Density altitude: \"Density altitude (DA) is pressure altitude corrected for nonstandard temperature deviations\u2026 Density altitude is not a height reference; rather, it is an index to aircraft performance. \" Choice A defines pressure altitude. Choice D is false: on a cold day the DA is lower than field elevation.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.180"},
    {q:"A weather balloon measures a temperature profile that is constant with increasing altitude through a layer. This layer is best described as:", o:["An inversion, which is a stable layer", "The standard lapse rate, since 2\u00b0 C/1,000 ft is an average", "Isothermal, and part of the environmental lapse rate measured by the balloon", "Adiabatic, since no heat was added or removed"], a:2, r:"Isothermal, part of the environmental lapse rate. SG, Lapse Rates: \"An isothermal lapse rate indicates the temperature is the same at different altitudes, \" and \"The resulting temperature profile [from the weather balloon] is known as the environmental lapse rate. \" An inversion (A) requires temperature to increase with altitude. Adiabatic (D) describes a process, not a measured profile.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.176 / 2.294"},
    {q:"Air can reach saturation (RH = 100%) by which of the following?", o:["Cooling the air, which lowers the dew point toward the air temperature", "Evaporation only, since cooling does not change the dew point spread", "Warming the air, which increases its capacity to hold water vapor", "Cooling the air, which lowers the air temperature toward the dew point; or by evaporation, which raises the"], a:3, r:"SG, Atmospheric Moisture: \"If the air is cooled, the falling air temperature decreases the dew point spread closer to zero\u2026 If evaporation occurs, this adds moisture to the atmosphere, increasing the dew point, which again lowers the dew point spread. \" Choice A gets the mechanism backwards \u2014 cooling lowers the air temperature toward the dew point, not the dew point toward the air temperature.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.197 / 2.295"},
    {q:"Which one of the following statements about dew point is most correct?", o:["The dew point can be higher than, equal to, or lower than the air temperature depending on relative humidity", "The dew point is a direct indication of the amount of moisture present in the air, and can never be higher than the air temperature", "A higher dew point indicates a drier air mass and a lower chance of cloud formation", "The dew point depression is the difference between the dew point and the standard day temperature"], a:1, r:"SG, Atmospheric Moisture: \"The dew point is a direct indication of the amount of moisture present in the air\u2026 The dew point can never be higher than the air temperature: only equal to or less than. \" C reverses the meaning of a high dew point; D misdefines dew point depression (it is the spread between air temperature and dew point, not the standard day temperature).", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.198"},
    {q:"Air masses are named according to which three properties?", o:["Location, moisture content, and temperature", "Location, stability, and pressure", "Moisture content, temperature, and stability", "Latitude, altitude, and season"], a:0, r:"SG, Air Masses: \"Air masses are named according to their location, moisture content, and temperature. \" Stability is a consequence of temperature, not a naming category \u2014 which is what makes B and C tempting.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.184"},
    {q:"What is the primary reason atmospheric circulation takes place?", o:["The rotation of the Earth about its vertical axis", "The Coriolis force acting on air already in motion", "Ascending and descending currents caused by constant heating and cooling", "Magnetic variation between different regions of the Earth"], a:2, r:"SG, Atmospheric Circulation: \"Atmospheric circulation moves air in mainly two ways: ascending and descending currents. Because of the constant heating and cooling, the currents are always happening. \" Earth's rotation and the Coriolis force explain why the wind curves, not why circulation exists in the first place.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.293"},
    {q:"Surface analysis charts reference all reported pressures to mean sea level rather than to station pressure because:", o:["Station pressure cannot be measured accurately at high-elevation stations", "Isobars can only be drawn at 4-millibar intervals at sea level", "Sea level pressure is the value set in the Kollsman window at all altitudes", "Station pressures would cause the chart to depict the inverse of land topography, with lows always over"], a:3, r:"SG, Station and Sea Level Pressure: \"If, instead, station pressures were used, the pressure charts would depict the inverse of the land topography\u2026 Mountain tops would always have lows over them, and valleys would have highs. \"", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.179"},
    {q:"The wind at 3,000 feet AGL is from the north and is the result of a high pressure system. The surface wind in the same area would be from approximately the:", o:["Northeast", "Southwest", "Southeast", "Northwest"], a:3, r:"Northwest. Above 2,000 ft AGL the wind is a gradient wind flowing parallel to the isobars; at the surface, friction reduces the Coriolis force so the wind is turned back toward lower pressure by 45\u00b0 , i.e., outward from a high. A gradient wind from the north is moving toward the south; rotating that motion 45\u00b0 counterclockwise (outward from the high) points it toward the southeast, meaning it comes from the northwest. SG, Surface Winds: surface winds \"still move clockwise around highs\u2026 but since they blow across the isobars at a 45\u00b0 angle, they also have a component of motion that moves air out of the high pressure. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.186"},
    {q:"A station lies due south of the center of a low pressure system, above 2,000 feet AGL. The gradient wind at that station will be from approximately the:", o:["North", "South", "East", "West"], a:3, r:"From the west. Gradient winds flow counterclockwise around a low, parallel to the isobars. At a point due south of the low center, counterclockwise flow is directed toward the east \u2014 so the wind is coming from the west. Cross-check with Buys Ballot's Law: with the wind at your back (facing east), the low is to your left \u2014 due north \u2014 which is exactly where the low is. (Caution: a widely circulated legacy practice test keys this scenario \"from the east. \" That key contradicts both Buys Ballot's Law and the SG's own statement that circulation around a low is counterclockwise; the legacy explanation even says \"diverted to the right, so going West, \" which is not a right-hand deflection from a northward flow. Trust the SG.)", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.185"},
    {q:"Compared to summer, the wintertime jet stream is:", o:["Farther north, at a higher altitude, and slower", "Farther north, at a lower altitude, and faster", "Farther south, at a higher altitude, and slower", "Farther south, at a lower altitude, and faster"], a:3, r:"SG, The Jet Stream: \"During the winter, the position of the jet stream is further south, the core descends to lower altitudes, and its speed is faster than in the summer. \" All three attributes must be right \u2014 each wrong answer gets exactly one of them wrong.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.187"},
    {q:"A crew encounters jet stream turbulence. After beginning an altitude change they observe the outside air temperature rising. Which combination of actions is recommended?", o:["Turn north and descend", "Turn south and descend", "Turn south and climb", "Turn north and climb"], a:2, r:"Turn south and climb. SG, Jet Stream Turbulence: \"The horizontal shear on the cold air side of the core is stronger than on the warm air side. Thus, if it is desired to exit jet stream turbulence, a turn to the south should result in smoother air\u2026 If the temperature is rising, climb; if the temperature is falling, descend. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.211 / 2.212"},
    {q:"A cloud with a base at 7,000 feet AGL and a top at 21,000 feet AGL is classified as a:", o:["Low cloud, because the base is below 10,000 feet", "Special cloud with extensive vertical development", "High cloud, because the top extends above 20,000 feet", "Middle cloud, because classification is determined by the height of the base"], a:3, r:"SG, Types of Clouds: \"The height of the cloud base, not the top, determines the classification. \" A base at 7,000 ft AGL falls in the 6,500\u201320,000 ft middle cloud band. Choice C is the trap for anyone who reads the top.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.196"},
    {q:"Which statement about high clouds is correct?", o:["They pose a significant icing hazard because they contain super-cooled water droplets", "They produce steady precipitation but no turbulence", "They have little effect on flying except for moderate turbulence and limited visibility in dense jet stream cirrus, and are not an icing hazard", "They frequently hide terrain and produce low ceilings"], a:2, r:"SG, High Clouds: \"High clouds have little effect on flying except for moderate turbulence and limited visibility associated with dense jet stream cirrus. Since high clouds are composed mostly of ice crystals, they have no precipitation and do not constitute an icing hazard. \" (Severe/extreme turbulence is found in thunderstorm anvil cirrus, which is a separate case.)", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.196"},
    {q:"With respect to an occluded front, the most severe weather \u2014 including the lowest ceilings and visibilities \u2014 is generally located:", o:["100 NM north to 300 NM south of the frontal intersection", "500 to 700 NM east of the surface front", "Within 50 NM of the surface front in all directions", "100 NM south to 300 NM north of the frontal intersection"], a:3, r:"SG, Occluded Fronts: \"the most severe weather, including ceilings and visibilities, is generally located in an area 100 NM south to 300 NM north of the frontal intersection. \" Choice A reverses the two figures.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"Which statement correctly distinguishes a warm front occlusion?", o:["The cold front remains in contact with the ground and undercuts the cool air ahead of the warm front", "It produces exclusively cold front type weather regardless of the direction of approach", "It is depicted in red on surface analysis charts and moves toward the southeast", "The air behind the cold front is not as cold as the cool air mass ahead of the warm front, so the cold air behind"], a:3, r:"SG, Occluded Fronts: \"Warm occlusions occur when the air behind the \u2018cold' front is actually not as cold as the cool air mass ahead of the warm front\u2026 the colder, denser air mass ahead of the warm front remains at the surface, forcing the cold air mass behind the cold front up as well. \" Choice A describes a cold occlusion. Choice C is wrong twice \u2014 occlusions are purple and move NE.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"During steady warm frontal rain, ceilings are often in which range, and precipitation typically begins where the middle cloud deck is at what height?", o:["300 to 900 feet; 8,000 to 12,000 feet AGL", "900 to 1,500 feet; 6,500 to 20,000 feet AGL", "300 to 900 feet; 500 to 700 feet AGL", "1,000 to 3,000 feet; 8,000 to 12,000 feet MSL"], a:0, r:"SG, Warm Front Flight Problems: \"Ceilings are often in the 300 to 900-foot range during steady, warm frontal rain situations, \" and \"precipitation will begin where the middle cloud deck is from 8000 to 12,000 feet AGL. \" Choice C substitutes the 500\u2013700 mile cloud shield extent as a height.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"If an active cold front is moving at a moderate to rapid speed (15\u201330 knots), its weather zone is generally:", o:["Less than 50 miles wide, with generally VMC conditions and isolated IMC in heavy precipitation and near thunderstorms", "Less than 50 miles wide, with widespread IMC conditions throughout", "50 to 300 miles wide, with generally VMC conditions", "500 to 700 miles wide, with low stratus and fog"], a:0, r:"SG, Cold Fronts Flight Problems: \"If an active cold front moves at a moderate or rapid speed (15-30 knots), its weather zone is generally less than 50 miles wide\u2026 Ceilings and visibilities are generally visual meteorological conditions (VMC), but isolated instrument meteorological conditions (IMC) exist in heavy precipitation and near thunderstorms. \" B has the right width but the wrong flight conditions.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"A pilot flying through a front notes that temperature, dew point, and wind change abruptly over a very short distance. This indicates:", o:["A broad, diffuse frontal zone often over 200 miles wide, with generally milder weather", "That the aircraft has crossed the upper front rather than the surface front", "A stationary front, since only stationary fronts produce abrupt changes", "A narrow frontal zone, in some cases less than a mile wide, associated with more severe weather"], a:3, r:"SG, Frontal Systems: \"Abrupt changes indicate that the frontal zone is narrow, and in some cases, the zone can be less than a mile wide\u2026 Abrupt changes will bring more severe weather than gradual changes. \" Choice A describes the opposite case (gradual changes, broad zone over 200 miles).", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.190 / 2.191"},
    {q:"At approximately what altitude do the differences between the two air masses forming a front generally become negligible?", o:["Above 6,500 to 10,000 feet", "Above 15,000 to 20,000 feet", "Above 25,000 to 30,000 feet", "At the tropopause, in all cases"], a:1, r:"SG, General Frontal Structure: \"At some level above the surface, usually above 15,000 to 20,000 feet, the differences between the two air masses forming the front become negligible. \" Choice D is wrong as an absolute, though the SG notes the temperature contrast can sometimes extend as high as the tropopause.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.190"},
    {q:"Turbulence along a warm front is best described by which statement?", o:["Turbulence is widespread throughout the warm frontal cloud shield due to the shallow slope", "The only turbulence normally found along a warm front is in embedded thunderstorms; otherwise little to no turbulence exists", "Warm front turbulence is generally more severe than cold front turbulence because of the greater areal extent", "Turbulence is caused by the abrupt 90\u00b0 wind shift, which is more sudden than that of a cold front"], a:1, r:"SG, Turbulence and Thunderstorms (warm front): \"The only turbulence along a warm front would be found in such embedded thunderstorms. Otherwise, little to no turbulence exists in warm front systems. \" Choice D is doubly wrong \u2014 the warm front wind shift is less sudden than a cold front's, and it is a 90\u00b0 shift from SE to SW.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"Which one of the following correctly states the extent of thunderstorm-related turbulence?", o:["Turbulence can extend over 5,000 feet above the cloud tops and 20 miles laterally, with severe turbulence in the anvil 15 to 30 miles downwind", "Turbulence can extend over 1,000 feet above the cloud tops and 5 miles laterally, with severe turbulence in the anvil 5 to 10 miles downwind", "Turbulence is confined to the visible cloud and the area directly beneath the cloud base", "Turbulence extends 5,000 feet above the tops but only within the precipitation shaft"], a:0, r:"SG, Turbulence Associated with Thunderstorms: \"wind shear turbulence has been encountered several thousand feet above and 20 miles laterally from a severe storm. Severe turbulence can be encountered in the anvil 15 to 30 miles downwind, \" and Extreme Turbulence: \"This turbulence can extend over 5000 feet above the cloud tops and down to the ground beneath the cloud base. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.223"},
    {q:"Which one of the following radar separation rules is stated correctly?", o:["Avoid severe or intense echoes by at least 20 miles; echoes should be separated by at least 40 miles before flying between them; severe CAT and hail may be experienced between storms if echo separation is less than 30 miles", "Avoid severe echoes by at least 40 miles; echoes should be separated by at least 20 miles before flying between them; severe CAT may occur if separation is less than 20 miles", "Avoid severe echoes by at least 10 miles; echoes should be separated by at least 30 miles before flying between them", "Avoid severe echoes by at least 20 miles; echoes should be separated by at least 30 miles; hail is not a concern"], a:0, r:"SG, Flight Techniques in the Vicinity of Thunderstorms, items 14 and 3: \"Avoid by at least 20 mi any thunderstorm identified as severe\u2026 Such echoes should be separated by at least 40 mi before flying between echoes, \" and \"Severe clear air turbulence and hail may be experienced between thunderstorms if the separation between echoes is less than 30 miles. \" Three different numbers \u2014 20, 40, 30 \u2014 each attached to a different rule. B swaps the 20 and 40.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.225"},
    {q:"A thunderstorm with radar echo tops indicated above ______ often contains extreme turbulence and hail, and should be regarded as extremely hazardous.", o:["25,000 feet", "30,000 feet", "35,000 feet", "45,000 feet"], a:2, r:"35,000 feet. SG, RADAR information: \"A thunderstorm with radar echo tops indicated above 35,000 feet often contains extreme turbulence and hail, \" and item 17: \"Regard as extremely hazardous any thunderstorm with tops 35,000 ft or higher. \" (45,000 ft is the altitude at which hail has been encountered in clear air.)", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.223"},
    {q:"A thunderstorm cell is considered to have entered the mature stage when:", o:["The updraft exceeds 3,000 feet per minute", "The anvil first becomes visible", "Precipitation reaches the surface", "Subsiding air replaces the updraft throughout the cloud"], a:2, r:"SG, Mature Stage: \"The cell transitions to the mature stage when precipitation reaches the surface. \" A describes the towering cumulus stage; D describes the dissipating stage; B is explicitly unreliable \u2014 \"the appearance of an anvil does not indicate the thunderstorm is free of hazards. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.223"},
    {q:"Which set of microburst parameters is stated correctly?", o:["Downward velocities of 2,000 to over 6,000 fpm; differential velocities of 20 to 200 knots; diameter of \u00bc to 2\u00bd miles; life span of 5 to 10 minutes after reaching the ground", "Downward velocities of 2,000 to over 6,000 fpm; differential velocities of 20 to 50 knots; diameter of 2 to 5 miles; life span of 20 minutes to 1\u00bd hours", "Downward velocities of 200 to 600 fpm; differential velocities of 20 to 200 knots; diameter of \u00bc to 2\u00bd miles; life span of 5 to 10 minutes", "Downward velocities of 2,000 to over 6,000 fpm; differential velocities of 20 to 200 knots; diameter of 5 to 10"], a:0, r:"SG, Microbursts: \"velocities of 2000 to over 6000 feet per minute\u2026 producing a vortex ring of wind that can produce differential velocities ranging from 20 to 200 knots in an area only \u00bc to 2\u00bd miles in diameter\u2026 its short life span\u2013usually only 5 to 10 minutes after reaching the ground. \" Choice B substitutes the thunderstorm cell life cycle (20 min to 1\u00bd hr).", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.224"},
    {q:"An aircraft on final approach enters a microburst and the crew observes an increasing headwind, rising indicated airspeed, and a pitch-up tendency. The correct response is to:", o:["Reduce power and apply nose-down stick to hold the glidepath, since the airspeed excess will dissipate", "Extend additional drag devices to bleed off the excess airspeed", "Maintain the current power setting and allow the aircraft to stabilize on its own", "Anticipate the imminent transition to a strong tailwind and downdraft, and add maximum power while"], a:3, r:"SG, Microbursts (Figure 3\u201121): the pilot's natural reaction of reducing power and pushing the nose down \"would correct the situation if the aircraft was not in a microburst, \" but at position 3\u20134 the headwind becomes a strong tailwind in a severe downdraft, and \"the correct reaction would be to add maximum power and establish a climbing attitude on the vertical gyro. \" Choice A is the instinctive \u2014 and fatal \u2014 response.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.224"},
    {q:"Most aircraft lightning strikes occur under which conditions?", o:["Within 8\u00b0 C of the freezing level, within approximately 5,000 feet of the freezing level, in precipitation, in clouds, and in some turbulence", "Within 8\u00b0 C of the freezing level, within approximately 1,000 feet of the freezing level, and only inside cumulonimbus clouds", "Above 43,000 feet, in clear air, and outside of precipitation", "Only within the anvil, and only while the storm is in the mature stage"], a:0, r:"SG, Aircraft Lightning or ESD Encounters: strikes occur \"1. Within 8\u00b0 C of the freezing level; 2. Within approximately 5000 feet of the freezing level; 3. In precipitation, including snow; 4. In clouds; 5. In some turbulence. \" Note the SG's caveat that not all of these need occur \u2014 but 5,000 ft, not 1,000 ft, is the stated figure. Choice C misuses the 43,000-foot figure, which is the highest altitude at which strikes have been recorded.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.223"},
    {q:"Which statement about icing and cloud temperature is correct?", o:["Icing is impossible below \u201320\u00b0 C because all clouds are ice crystals at that temperature", "Serious icing is rare in clouds colder than \u201320\u00b0 C since these clouds are almost completely composed of ice crystals, but icing is possible in any cloud when the temperature is 0\u00b0 C or below", "Water droplets in free air freeze at 0\u00b0 C, so icing is certain in any cloud below freezing", "The larger the droplet, the lower its freezing point"], a:1, r:"SG, Icing Requirements: \"As a general rule, serious icing is rare in clouds with temperatures below \u201320\u00b0 C since these clouds are almost completely composed of ice crystals. However, be aware that icing is possible in any cloud when the temperature is 0\u00b0 C or below. \" Choice D is backwards: \"the smaller the droplets, the lower the freezing point. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.214 / 2.215"},
    {q:"Compressor icing is indicated to the pilot by:", o:["A loss of thrust and a rapid rise in exhaust gas temperature", "A loss of thrust and a rapid drop in exhaust gas temperature", "An increase in thrust and a decrease in the fuel-air ratio", "Erroneous airspeed indications with no change in engine parameters"], a:0, r:"SG, Compressor icing: \"The reduction in airflow is noticeable through a loss of thrust and a rapid rise in exhaust gas temperature. As the airflow decreases, the fuel-air ratio increases. \" Choices B and C each invert one of these two indications.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.217"},
    {q:"Regarding the aerodynamic effect of ice on an airfoil, which statement is correct?", o:["Ice thickness is the only significant factor in determining the loss of lift", "Distributed sandpaper-like roughness on the leading edge has a negligible effect on lift", "A half-inch ridge of ice at 50% chord reduces maximum lift by over 50%, while the same ridge at 4% chord reduces it by only about 15%", "A half-inch ridge of ice at 4% chord reduces maximum lift by over 50%, while the same ridge at 50% chord"], a:3, r:"SG, Effects and Hazards of Structural Icing: \"a half-inch high ridge of ice on the upper surface of the airfoil at 4% chord reduces maximum lift by over 50%. Yet, the same ridge of ice at 50% chord decreases maximum lift by only 15%. \" The SG explicitly states that \"Ice thickness is not the only factor\u2026 Location, roughness, and shape are important, too, \" and that leading-edge roughness alone may cost 35% of lift.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.216"},
    {q:"Induction icing can occur in conditions of:", o:["High humidity at temperatures up to +10\u00b0 C", "Freezing temperatures with visible moisture only", "Cumulus clouds colder than -20\u00b0 C only", "Low humidity at any temperature below standard"], a:0, r:"Induction icing forms in the air intake duct, where reduced pressure lowers the temperature enough for condensation and freezing \u2014 so it can occur in high humidity at outside air temperatures up to +10\u00b0 C, well above freezing. That above-freezing detail is the tested point.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.217"},
    {q:"Advection fog dissipates:", o:["Primarily through solar heating shortly after sunrise", "Only with a change in wind direction that moves the air from a colder surface to a warmer surface", "When winds decrease below 5 knots", "When the dew point spread increases due to nocturnal cooling"], a:1, r:"SG, Advection Fog: \"Advection fog dissipates only with a wind shift\u2026 Incoming solar radiation will seldom cause the dissipation of advection fog\u2026 Only a change in wind direction that moves the air from a colder surface to a warmer surface, reversing the saturation process, can cause advection fog to dissipate. \" Choice A is how radiation fog dissipates. Choice C is backwards \u2014 advection fog gets thicker as wind increases up to about 15 knots.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.222"},
    {q:"Fog that extends no more than 200 feet in height is considered shallow fog. One of the most serious problems it presents on approach is that:", o:["It normally causes a total obscuration, so no visual cues appear at any point", "There is an abundance of visual cues early in the approach, and the subsequent loss of those cues upon entering the fog layer may cause confusion or disorientation", "It reduces vertical visibility to zero while leaving slant range visibility unaffected", "It is always reported as a total obscuration with a vertical visibility group"], a:1, r:"SG, Fog vs Stratus: \"One of the most serious problems with shallow fog stems from the abundance of cues available at the start of the approach\u2026 as the fog level is entered, loss of visual cues may cause confusion or disorientation. \" Choice A describes dense fog (total obscuration); shallow fog \"is normally reported as a partial obscuration, \" so D is also wrong.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.219"},
    {q:"An observer can see 3 miles throughout 200\u00b0 of the horizon circle, but a bank of fog to the east limits visibility to \u00bd mile over the remaining 160\u00b0. The prevailing visibility is:", o:["\u00bd mile", "1\u00be miles, the average of the two sectors", "3 miles", "Not reportable, since the horizon circle is not continuous"], a:2, r:"3 miles. SG, Visibility Definitions: prevailing visibility is \"the greatest horizontal visibility\u2026 equaled or exceeded throughout at least half the horizon circle, which need not be continuous. \" 200\u00b0 exceeds 180\u00b0 , so 3 miles is the prevailing visibility even though a large sector is much worse. Prevailing visibility is never an average (B), and the SG's Figure 4\u20111 uses exactly this fog-bank scenario.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.226"},
    {q:"A reported vertical visibility of 200 feet differs from a reported cloud ceiling of 200 feet because:", o:["Vertical visibility is measured in feet MSL while a ceiling is measured in feet AGL", "With a cloud ceiling, a pilot can normally expect to see the ground and runway upon descending below the base; with vertical visibility, the obscuring phenomenon also degrades slant range visibility, so the runway may still be difficult to see", "Vertical visibility is reported only for partial obscurations, which are not ceilings", "A vertical visibility of 200 feet indicates better visual conditions than a 200-foot cloud ceiling"], a:1, r:"SG, Sky Coverage and Ceilings: \"With a low cloud ceiling, a pilot normally can expect to see the ground and the runway once the aircraft descends below the cloud base. However, in the case of vertical visibility, the obscuring phenomenon also reduces the slant range visibility. Therefore, a pilot will have difficulty seeing the runway or approach lights clearly even after descending below the level of the reported vertical visibility. \" Both are reported in feet AGL, so A is false; a vertical visibility report is a total obscuration and does constitute a ceiling, so C is false.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.227"},
    {q:"A TAF contains the icing group 641104. This forecasts:", o:["Moderate icing from 11,000 feet AGL to 15,000 feet AGL", "Severe icing from 11,000 feet AGL to 14,000 feet AGL", "Moderate icing from 1,100 feet AGL to 5,100 feet AGL", "Moderate icing from 11,000 feet MSL, thickness unspecified"], a:0, r:"SG, Icing Group: 6=icing forecast; 4=moderate icing; 110=base at 11,000 ft AGL; 4=layer 4,000 ft thick. Base 11,000 + thickness 4,000 = tops at 15,000 ft AGL. Choice B misreads the intensity digit; choice C misplaces the decimal on the base height.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.25"},
    {q:"A TAF contains the group 530004. This forecasts:", o:["Light turbulence from the surface to 4,000 feet AGL", "Moderate turbulence in clear air from the surface to 4,000 feet AGL", "Severe icing from the surface to 4,000 feet AGL", "Moderate turbulence from 3,000 feet to 4,000 feet AGL"], a:1, r:"SG, Turbulence Group: 5=turbulence forecast; 3=moderate turbulence in clear air (per Table 4\u20117); 000=base at or below 100 ft AGL (surface); 4=4,000 ft thick. The SG decodes this exact group in its TAF example as \"moderate turbulence in clear air from surface up to 4000 feet. \" A group beginning with 6 would be icing, so C is a category error.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.25"},
    {q:"On a Winds-Aloft Forecast, the code 7409 decodes to:", o:["740\u00b0 at 09 knots \u2014 a coding error", "240\u00b0 at 109 knots", "740\u00b0 at 109 knots", "240\u00b0 at 09 knots"], a:1, r:"240\u00b0 T at 109 knots. SG, Special Circumstances 3: for winds of 100 knots or greater, 500 is added to the direction and 100 subtracted from the speed. To decode, subtract 500 from the \"unrealistic\" direction (740 \u2013 500 = 240) and add 100 to the speed (09 + 100 = 109). The SG uses 7409 as its own worked example.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.27"},
    {q:"Which one of the following correctly states the Winds-Aloft Forecast omission rules?", o:["Wind is never forecast within 1,500 feet of the surface; temperature is never forecast within 2,500 feet of the surface; temperature is never forecast for the 3,000-foot level", "Wind is never forecast within 2,500 feet of the surface; temperature is never forecast within 1,500 feet of the surface", "Wind and temperature are both omitted within 3,000 feet of the surface", "Temperature is omitted above 24,000 feet, since all temperatures are negative there"], a:0, r:"SG, Special Circumstances 4: \"Wind information is never forecast for altitudes within 1500 feet of the surface. Temperature information is never forecast within 2500 feet of the surface. Temperature information is never forecast for the 3000-foot level. \" Choice B swaps the two figures; choice D inverts the rule \u2014 above 24,000 ft temperatures are forecast, and are simply all negative.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.27"},
    {q:"Which statement about Convective SIGMETs is correct?", o:["They are issued every 6 hours and are valid for 6 hours", "They are issued hourly at H+55, the forecast is valid for up to 2 hours, and any Convective SIGMET implies severe or greater turbulence, severe icing, and low-level wind shear", "They are issued hourly and imply only moderate turbulence and moderate icing", "They are issued only when a tornado has been observed"], a:1, r:"SG, US Convective SIGMETs: \"Bulletins are issued hourly at Hour+55\u2026 The forecast is valid for up to 2 hours, \" and \"Any convective SIGMET implies severe or greater turbulence, severe icing, and low level wind shear. \" Choice A gives the non-convective SIGMET timing (4 hours, or 6 for hurricanes).", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.229"},
    {q:"A G-AIRMET depicting areas of moderate airframe icing and the freezing level is designated:", o:["SIERRA", "TANGO", "ZULU", "Convective SIGMET"], a:2, r:"ZULU. SG, G-AIRMET: \"Icing and Freezing Level AIRMETs are ZULU AIRMETs. \" TANGO covers turbulence, LLWS, and strong surface winds; SIERRA covers IFR conditions and mountain obscuration.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.229"},
    {q:"Rain that evaporates before reaching the ground \u2014 often one of the first visual cues of a microburst \u2014 is called:", o:["Virga", "Drizzle", "A rain shaft", "Graupel"], a:0, r:"Virga is precipitation that does not reach the ground. Along with localized blowing dust, rain shafts, and roll clouds, it is a visual cue that a microburst may be present or imminent.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.224"},
    {q:"A BECMG group in a TAF indicates a change occurring over a period that is normally about ______ and at most ______.", o:["2 hours; 4 hours", "1 hour; 2 hours", "4 hours; 8 hours", "Instantaneous; 1 hour"], a:0, r:"about 2 hours, at most 4. SG, BECMG Group: \"The duration of this change is normally about 2 hours, 4 hours at most and is how long the weather depicted within the BECMG line will take to fully develop. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.25"},
    {q:"When using data-linked NEXRAD mosaic imagery in flight, a pilot must remember that:", o:["The imagery is real time and may be used as the sole means of tactical maneuvering through a thunderstorm area", "The imagery shows where the weather was, not where it is, and conditions may be 15 to 20 minutes older than the age indicated on the display", "The imagery reliably detects microbursts out to 100 NM from the radar site", "Echoes rarely change shape or intensity in less than one hour"], a:1, r:"SG, Flight Techniques in the Vicinity of Thunderstorms, item 8: \"Remember that the data-linked NEXRAD mosaic imagery shows where the weather was, not where the weather is. The weather conditions may be 15 to 20 minutes older than the age indicated on the display. \" Choice A contradicts the SG's guidance on tactical maneuvering; choice C misstates the microburst detection limit, which is about 20 NM from the radar site; choice D contradicts the SG's warning that echoes \"can change shape, character, and intensity in a matter of minutes. \" APPENDIX \u2014 LEGACY PRACTICE-TEST ITEMS EXCLUDED AFTER CROSS-CHECK The following items appear in the older practice tests supplied but are not supported by the January 2025 Student Guide and were deliberately left out of all three tests: Legacy item Tropical disturbance / tropical cyclone wind thresholds; Gale and Storm Warning Conditions I\u2013III Frontogenesis / frontolysis Air mass letter codes (mTk, cPw, mPk, cTk, etc.) \"Water vapor is 0 to 10% of the atmosphere by volume\" Middle clouds \"5,500\u201318,500 ft\" ; high clouds \"above 18,500 ft\" \"Fog base at or lower than 50 feet\u2026 (True)\" as a standalone T/F Why it was excluded No tropical cyclone or warning-condition material appears anywhere in the Jan 2025 SG. Neither term appears in the Jan 2025 SG. The Jan 2025 SG names air masses by location, moisture content, and temperature but does not teach the letter-code shorthand. Jan 2025 SG states 0% to 5% by volume. Jan 2025 SG uses 6,500\u201320,000 ft AGL (middle) and above 20,000 ft AGL (high). Jan 2025 SG requires all three parameters: based at or within 50 ft of the surface, greater than 20 ft in depth, and prevailing visibility less than \u215d SM. The legacy T/F omits the depth and visibility criteria. \"Most severe icing between +2\u00b0 C and \u201310\u00b0 C\" Station pressure problem keyed as 1130.0 mb Gradient wind at a station south of a low keyed as \"from the east\" \"Five factors that influence frontal weather\" (slope, stability, moisture, speed, contrast) as a numbered list T-34C / carburetor-icing-specific engine questions Turbulence intensity \"Trace\" End of Test 3. Jan 2025 SG states clear icing occurs 0\u00b0 C to \u201310\u00b0 C; rime \u201310 to \u201320; mixed \u20138 to \u201315. No \"+2\u00b0 C\" figure exists. Physically and doctrinally wrong \u2014 station pressure must be lower than sea level pressure. Correct answer is 960.0 mb (used as Test 2, Q6). Contradicts counterclockwise circulation around a low and Buys Ballot's Law. Correct answer is from the west (used as Test 3, Q15). The Jan 2025 SG discusses slope, speed, moisture, and contrast, but does not present them as an enumerated set of five, so a \"which two of the five\" question is not testable as written. The Jan 2025 SG discusses induction and compressor icing generically and does not tie engine icing to a specific airframe. Trace is an icing intensity. Turbulence intensities are light, moderate, severe, extreme. (Retained, but only as a distractor \u2014 see Test 1, Q28.)", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.28"},
  ]
},
{
  name: "Topic 1 \u2014 Theory",
  desc: "Chapter-focused review: atmosphere, pressure, altimetry, lapse rates, moisture, and air masses.",
  questions: [
    {q:"According to the Student Guide, the height of the troposphere varies from an average of:", o:["36,000 feet MSL over the equator to 18,000 feet MSL over the poles", "55,000 feet MSL over the equator to 28,000 feet MSL over the poles", "28,000 feet MSL over the equator to 55,000 feet MSL over the poles", "53,000 feet MSL over the equator to 36,000 feet MSL over the poles"], a:1, r:"SG: \"It varies in height from an average 55,000 feet MSL over the equator to 28,000 feet MSL over the poles.\" Trap: option D borrows the real numbers 53,000 (90% of atmosphere by weight) and 36,000 (average height over the U.S.) and puts them in the equator/pole slots.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.172"},
    {q:"Which one of the following statements about the troposphere is correct?", o:["Roughly 90% of the atmosphere by weight lies below 18,000 feet.", "Winds are generally strongest near the Earth's surface and decrease with altitude.", "Within the troposphere, temperature normally increases with increasing altitude.", "Due to heating from the sun, the troposphere extends to a greater height in summer than in winter."], a:3, r:"SG: \"Due to heating from the sun, the troposphere extends to a greater height in summer than in winter.\" Trap: option A reverses the SG figures \u2014 it is \"roughly 50% of it, by weight, lies below 18,000 feet and 90% within 53,000 feet.\"", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.172"},
    {q:"Atmospheric (barometric) pressure is best defined as:", o:["the pressure exerted on a surface by the atmosphere due to the weight of the column of air directly above that surface", "the rate of pressure change in a direction perpendicular to the isobars", "the height of a column of mercury that can be supported at mean sea level on a standard day", "the percent of saturation of the air at a given temperature"], a:0, r:"SG: \"Atmospheric (barometric) pressure is the pressure exerted on a surface by the atmosphere due to the weight of the column of air directly above that surface.\" Trap: option B is the SG definition of the pressure gradient, not pressure itself.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.173"},
    {q:"Normal sea level pressures in the atmosphere vary from as low as:", o:["29.92 in-Hg (1013.2 mb) to as high as 31 in-Hg (about 1060 mb)", "28 in-Hg (about 1060 mb) to as high as 31 in-Hg (about 960 mb)", "28 in-Hg (about 960 mb) to as high as 31 in-Hg (about 1060 mb)", "27 in-Hg (about 900 mb) to as high as 32 in-Hg (about 1100 mb)"], a:2, r:"SG: \"Normal sea level pressures in the atmosphere vary from as low as 28 in-Hg (about 960 mb) to as high as 31 in-Hg (about 1060 mb).\" Trap: option B is the same correct in-Hg values with the millibar equivalents swapped.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.174"},
    {q:"Which one of the following statements about the units used to measure and report atmospheric pressure is correct?", o:["The millibar is a measure of the height of a column of mercury that can be supported by atmospheric pressure.", "Countries using the metric system report their altimeter settings in inches of mercury.", "Inches of mercury is a direct representation of pressure, defined as force per unit area.", "In the United States and Canada, altimeter settings are reported in inches of mercury."], a:3, r:"SG: \"However, in the United States and Canada altimeter settings are reported in inches of mercury.\" Trap: options A and C are the true SG statements reversed \u2014 inches of mercury measures the height of a mercury column, and the millibar is the direct representation of force per unit area.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.174"},
    {q:"On a surface analysis chart, the isobars over Sector A are packed closely together while the isobars over Sector B are widely spaced. Compared to Sector B, Sector A has:", o:["a shallow pressure gradient, a weak PGF, and slower winds", "a steep pressure gradient, a strong PGF, and faster winds", "a steep pressure gradient, a strong PGF, and slower winds", "a shallow pressure gradient, a strong PGF, and faster winds"], a:1, r:"SG: \"The PGF is steep, or strong, when isobars are close together, and is shallow, or weak, when the isobars are far apart\u2013the steeper the gradient, the stronger the winds.\" Trap: option C pairs the correct gradient description with the reversed wind result.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.175"},
    {q:"When air descends, the downward force against the Earth creates what at the surface, and what results as that air spreads out and diverges?", o:["A high-pressure system; the horizontal flow of air known as wind", "A low-pressure system; the horizontal flow of air known as wind", "A high-pressure system; the Coriolis force", "A low-pressure system; convergence toward the center of the high"], a:0, r:"SG: \"When the air descends, the downward force against the Earth creates a high-pressure system on the surface. The air then can only spread out and diverge... producing the horizontal flow of air known as wind.\" Trap: option B is the true statement for ascending air (\"Air moving upward... results in a low at the surface\"), attached to the wrong current.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.293"},
    {q:"A weather station sits at an elevation of 2,000 feet MSL and measures a station pressure of 27.92 in-Hg. Using the standard pressure lapse rate, its approximate sea level pressure is:", o:["25.92 in-Hg", "28.92 in-Hg", "29.92 in-Hg", "30.92 in-Hg"], a:2, r:"SG: \"a 1,000 foot increase in altitude will result in a pressure decrease of approximately 1 in-Hg (34 mb),\" and sea level pressure is \"calculated if the station is not at sea level using the standard pressure lapse rate.\" Arithmetic: 2,000 ft / 1,000 ft = 2 in-Hg; 27.92 + 2.00 = 29.92 in-Hg. Trap: option A subtracts instead of adds \u2014 going down to sea level, pressure increases.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.179"},
    {q:"Why do surface analysis charts use MSL, rather than station pressure, as the reference level for the depicted isobars?", o:["Because altimeters in the U.S. can only be set to mean sea level pressure", "So that daily pressure variations associated with weather systems can be tracked as they move across the country", "So that the isobars will accurately depict the contour lines of the land topography", "Because station pressure cannot be measured directly at high-elevation stations such as Denver"], a:1, r:"SG: \"This is done so that daily pressure variations associated with weather systems can be tracked as they move across the country.\" Trap: option C states the exact problem the MSL reference is meant to avoid \u2014 with station pressure \"the pressure charts would depict the inverse of the land topography.\"", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.179"},
    {q:"Standard day conditions at sea level are:", o:["29.92 in-Hg (1013.2 mb) and 15\u00b0 C (59\u00b0 F)", "29.92 in-Hg (1013.2 mb) and 59\u00b0 C (15\u00b0 F)", "30.00 in-Hg (1000.0 mb) and 15\u00b0 C (59\u00b0 F)", "28.00 in-Hg (960 mb) and 15\u00b0 C (59\u00b0 F)"], a:0, r:"SG: \"everything is related to standard day conditions at sea level, which are 29.92 in-Hg (1013.2 mb) and 15\u00b0 C (59\u00b0 F).\" Trap: option B is the correct pressure with the Celsius and Fahrenheit temperatures swapped.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.178"},
    {q:"A lapse rate is defined as:", o:["the rate of pressure change in a direction perpendicular to the isobars", "the temperature at which saturation occurs", "the difference between the air temperature and the dew point temperature", "the rate of change of an atmospheric variable, usually temperature, with height"], a:3, r:"SG: \"A lapse rate is the rate of change of an atmospheric variable, usually temperature, with height.\" Trap: option C is the dew point depression, and option B is the dew point temperature \u2014 both real definitions from this chapter, just not of a lapse rate.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.176"},
    {q:"The average, or standard, temperature lapse rate is:", o:["1.5\u00b0 C per 1,000 feet", "2\u00b0 C per 1,000 feet", "3.0\u00b0 C per 1,000 feet", "3.57\u00b0 C per 1,000 feet"], a:1, r:"SG: \"The average or standard lapse rate is 2\u00b0 Celsius (3.57\u00b0 Fahrenheit) per 1,000 feet.\" Trap: option D is the correct Fahrenheit figure mislabeled as Celsius.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.177"},
    {q:"The temperature at 3,000 feet MSL is +9\u00b0 C. Assuming the standard lapse rate, the approximate temperature at 12,000 feet MSL is:", o:["-3\u00b0 C", "-6\u00b0 C", "-9\u00b0 C", "-15\u00b0 C"], a:2, r:"SG: \"The average or standard lapse rate is 2\u00b0 Celsius... per 1,000 feet.\" Arithmetic: 12,000 - 3,000 = 9,000 ft of climb; 9 x 2\u00b0 C = 18\u00b0 C of cooling; +9\u00b0 C - 18\u00b0 C = -9\u00b0 C. Trap: option D (-15\u00b0 C) results from applying the lapse rate to the full 12,000 ft of indicated altitude (+9\u00b0 C - 24\u00b0 C) instead of to the 9,000 ft difference between the two levels.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.177"},
    {q:"Which one of the following best describes a temperature inversion?", o:["A lapse rate in which temperature increases with increasing altitude; stable conditions are generally found within it", "A lapse rate in which the temperature is the same at different altitudes; stable conditions are generally found within it", "A lapse rate in which temperature increases with increasing altitude; unstable conditions are generally found within it", "A layer several tens of thousands of feet thick in which temperature decreases rapidly with increasing altitude"], a:0, r:"SG: \"an inversion is a lapse rate where the temperature increases with increasing altitude... stable conditions are generally found within them.\" Trap: option B is the SG definition of an isothermal lapse rate (\"the temperature is the same at different altitudes\").", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.294"},
    {q:"Which one of the following statements about air masses is correct?", o:["An air mass has essentially uniform temperature and moisture conditions in the vertical plane.", "Air masses are named according to their location, moisture content, and altitude.", "Cold air masses bring stable conditions because colder air is moving over warmer surfaces.", "Warm air masses bring stable conditions, while cold air masses are inherently unstable."], a:3, r:"SG: \"Warm air masses bring stable conditions, while cold air masses are inherently unstable.\" Trap: option C states the true SG reasoning (\"cold air masses typically come from the north and consist of colder air moving over warmer surfaces\") but attaches it to the wrong stability outcome.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.184"},
    {q:"Air reaches saturation:", o:["when the relative humidity reaches 90 percent", "when the dew point temperature rises above the air temperature", "when it contains the maximum amount of water vapor it can hold for that temperature", "when condensation nuclei become numerous enough to appear as haze"], a:2, r:"SG: \"The air reaches saturation when it contains the maximum amount of water vapor it can hold for that temperature.\" Trap: option A cites the 90% RH figure, which the SG associates with a 2\u00b0 C / 4\u00b0 F spread \u2014 near saturation, not saturation.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.197"},
    {q:"Which one of the following statements about dew point temperature is correct?", o:["The dew point is the difference between the air temperature and the temperature at which saturation occurs.", "The dew point is a direct indication of the amount of moisture present in the air, and it can never be higher than the air temperature.", "The higher the dew point, the lower the chances for clouds, fog, or precipitation to form.", "In saturated air, the dew point temperature will be slightly higher than the air temperature."], a:1, r:"SG: \"The dew point is a direct indication of the amount of moisture present in the air,\" and \"The dew point can never be higher than the air temperature: only equal to or less than.\" Trap: option C reverses the SG rule \u2014 \"The higher the dew point, the greater chances for clouds, fog, or precipitation to form.\"", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.198"},
    {q:"A station reports a temperature of 18\u00b0 C and a dew point of 16\u00b0 C. According to the Student Guide, this observation indicates:", o:["a dew point depression of 2\u00b0 C, a relative humidity of about 90%, and water vapor beginning to condense into fog or clouds", "a dew point depression of 2\u00b0 C, a relative humidity of about 50%, and air that remains far from saturation", "a dew point depression of 4\u00b0 C, a relative humidity of about 90%, and air that remains far from saturation", "a dew point depression of 34\u00b0 C and a relative humidity of about 10%"], a:0, r:"SG: \"Once the dew point spread reaches about 2\u00b0 C / 4\u00b0 F, the RH will be 90%, and the water vapor will begin to condense into fog or clouds.\" Arithmetic: 18\u00b0 C - 16\u00b0 C = 2\u00b0 C dew point depression. Trap: option C uses the 4\u00b0 figure, which is the Fahrenheit equivalent of the same 2\u00b0 C spread, not a separate condition.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.198"},
    {q:"Relative humidity is:", o:["the amount of water vapor present in a given volume of air; it reaches 100% only through evaporation", "the percent of saturation of the air; it reaches 100% only through cooling", "the difference between the air temperature and the dew point; it reaches 100% when the air is warmed", "the percent of saturation of the air; it reaches 100% either by cooling the air or by adding moisture through evaporation"], a:3, r:"SG: \"relative humidity (RH), which is the percent of saturation of the air. The air can become saturated (RH = 100%) by one of two ways. If the air is cooled... If evaporation occurs...\" Trap: option B has the correct definition but names only one of the two saturation paths.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.295"},
    {q:"Pressure altitude is defined as:", o:["indicated altitude corrected for instrument error", "the actual height above mean sea level, found by correcting calibrated altitude for temperature deviations", "the height above the standard datum plane, the level at which the barometric pressure is 29.92 in-Hg", "pressure altitude corrected for nonstandard temperature deviations"], a:2, r:"SG: \"Pressure altitude (PA) is the height above the standard datum plane. The standard datum plane is the actual elevation above or below the earth's surface at which the barometric pressure is 29.92 in-Hg.\" Trap: option D is the SG definition of density altitude.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.180"},
    {q:"On a hot day, the density altitude at an airfield will be:", o:["higher than both the published field elevation and the pressure altitude, because air molecules are farther apart and the air density decreases", "lower than both the published field elevation and the pressure altitude, because air molecules are farther apart", "higher than the published field elevation, and it serves as a true height reference above mean sea level", "equal to the pressure altitude, since density altitude is not corrected for temperature"], a:0, r:"SG: \"On a hot day, air molecules are farther apart, decreasing the air density and increasing the density altitude. In this situation, the DA of an airfield would be higher than both the published field elevation and the pressure altitude.\" Trap: option C is correct on direction but wrong on use \u2014 \"Density altitude is not a height reference; rather, it is an index to aircraft performance.\"", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.180"},
    {q:"An aircraft's true altitude is 7,500 feet MSL over terrain with an elevation of 2,100 feet MSL. The aircraft's absolute altitude is:", o:["2,100 feet AGL", "5,400 feet AGL", "7,500 feet AGL", "9,600 feet AGL"], a:1, r:"SG: \"Absolute altitude... can be calculated by subtracting the terrain elevation from the true altitude.\" Arithmetic: 7,500 - 2,100 = 5,400 feet AGL. Trap: option D adds the two values instead of subtracting.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.180"},
    {q:"An aircraft assigned FL290 is flying:", o:["a true altitude of 29,000 feet MSL with the local altimeter setting in the Kollsman window", "an absolute altitude of 29,000 feet AGL as displayed on a radar altimeter", "a density altitude of 29,000 feet, corrected for nonstandard temperature", "a pressure altitude, flying the aircraft so the altimeter reads 29,000 feet with 29.92 in the Kollsman window"], a:3, r:"SG: \"an aircraft assigned FL250... would be flying a pressure altitude, and the pilot would fly the aircraft so that the altimeter reads 25,000 feet with 29.92 in the Kollsman window.\" Trap: option A is how altitudes work below 18,000 feet MSL, where the local altimeter setting is used.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.180"},
    {q:"Indicated altitude is:", o:["the altitude read directly from the altimeter", "indicated altitude corrected for instrument error", "the actual height above mean sea level", "the height above the standard datum plane"], a:0, r:"SG: \"Indicated altitude is the altitude read directly from the altimeter.\" Trap: option B is calibrated altitude \u2014 \"Calibrated altitude is indicated altitude corrected for instrument error.\"", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.181"},
    {q:"An aircraft is parked at an airfield with an elevation of 96 feet MSL with the local altimeter setting in the Kollsman window, and the altimeter indicates 20 feet. The instrument error is:", o:["-76 feet, and the aircraft is within limits for instrument flight", "+76 feet, and the aircraft is considered unsafe for instrument flight", "-76 feet, and the aircraft is considered unsafe for instrument flight", "-116 feet, and the aircraft is considered unsafe for instrument flight"], a:2, r:"SG: \"Instrument error is determined prior to takeoff by noting the difference between field elevation and indicated altitude,\" and \"If the instrument error is in excess of 75 ft, the aircraft is considered unsafe for instrument flight.\" Arithmetic: 20 ft indicated - 96 ft field elevation = -76 ft, which exceeds 75 ft. Trap: option A has the correct arithmetic but ignores the 75-foot limit \u2014 76 is in excess of 75.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.181"},
    {q:"An aircraft maintains a constant indicated altitude while the sea level pressure along its route decreases, all other conditions remaining the same. The aircraft will:", o:["climb to a higher AGL altitude as the column of air aloft is raised", "descend to a lower AGL altitude, because the whole column of air aloft is lowered", "maintain a constant AGL altitude, because it is flying along a surface of constant pressure", "maintain a constant AGL altitude, but only if 29.92 is set in the Kollsman window"], a:1, r:"SG: \"As the sea level pressure on the surface decreases (all other conditions remaining the same), the whole column of air aloft is lowered, causing an aircraft flying at an assigned MSL altitude to descend to a lower AGL altitude.\" Trap: option C is true about the pressure surface being flown but draws the wrong conclusion about AGL height.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.182"},
    {g:1, q:"A pilot correctly sets 29.70 in-Hg at the departure field and flies to a destination where the altimeter setting is 30.00 in-Hg, maintaining an assigned indicated altitude of 4,000 feet without resetting the altimeter. Assuming a standard lapse rate, the MSL/true altitude over the destination is approximately:", o:["3,700 feet", "4,000 feet", "4,030 feet", "4,300 feet"], a:3, r:"SG: \"Rule: Low to High, Plenty of Sky... MSL = Assigned Altitude (+) Altitude Error,\" and \"A change in pressure of 0.10 in-Hg will change the altimeter reading 100 feet.\" Arithmetic: 30.00 - 29.70 = 0.30 in-Hg; 0.30 / 0.10 x 100 = 300 ft error; 4,000 + 300 = 4,300 feet MSL. Trap: option A applies the \"High to Low, Look Out Below\" subtraction to a low-to-high flight.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.182"},
    {g:1, q:"Continuing the situation in the previous question, if the destination field elevation is 800 feet MSL and the pilot lands there without resetting the altimeter, the altimeter will indicate approximately:", o:["0 feet", "300 feet", "500 feet", "1,100 feet"], a:2, r:"SG: \"Lower Pressure to Higher Pressure... Indicated Altitude on Deck = Field Elevation (-) Altitude Error.\" Arithmetic: 800 ft field elevation - 300 ft error = 500 feet indicated on deck. Trap: option D adds the error instead of subtracting, which is the high-to-low case.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.182"},
    {q:"An aircraft flies from warm air into air that is colder than the standard atmosphere, and no temperature correction is applied. The altimeter will:", o:["read too high; the aircraft is actually lower than the altimeter indicates", "read too low; the aircraft is actually higher than the altimeter indicates", "read correctly, because altimeters are calibrated to the environmental lapse rate", "read too high; the aircraft is actually higher than the altimeter indicates"], a:0, r:"SG: \"As you fly from warm to cold air, an altimeter will read too high\u2013the aircraft is lower than the altimeter indicates.\" Trap: option D pairs the correct \"read too high\" with the reversed aircraft position.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.183"},
    {q:"An aircraft indicates 10,000 feet in air that is 22\u00b0 C colder than standard. Approximately what is the altimeter error, and what is the resulting true altitude?", o:["400 feet error; true altitude approximately 9,600 feet", "800 feet error; true altitude approximately 9,200 feet", "800 feet error; true altitude approximately 10,800 feet", "220 feet error; true altitude approximately 9,780 feet"], a:1, r:"SG: \"For every 11 \u00b0C that the temperature varies from the standard, the altimeter will be in error by 4%,\" and \"If the air is colder than the standard atmosphere, the aircraft will be lower than the altimeter indicates.\" Arithmetic: 22 / 11 = 2; 2 x 4% = 8%; 8% of 10,000 ft = 800 ft; colder than standard, so true altitude = 10,000 - 800 = 9,200 feet. Trap: option C uses the correct 800-foot magnitude but applies it in the warmer-than-standard direction.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.183"},
  ]
},
{
  name: "Topic 2 \u2014 Mechanics",
  desc: "Chapter-focused review: winds, jet stream, clouds, lifting, stability, and frontal systems.",
  questions: [
    {q:"The pressure gradient force and the Coriolis force together produce the gradient winds. Which statement correctly describes those winds?", o:["They flow perpendicular to the isobars, directly from high pressure toward low pressure.", "They flow parallel to the isobars \u2014 clockwise around highs and counterclockwise around lows \u2014 and are found above 2,000 feet AGL.", "They flow across the isobars at about a 45\u00b0 angle and are found below 2,000 feet AGL.", "They flow parallel to the isobars but circulate counterclockwise around highs and clockwise around lows."], a:1, r:"SG: \"gradient winds flow parallel to the isobars (Figure 2-2), and the resulting circulation flows clockwise around highs, and counterclockwise around lows. Finally, gradient winds are found above 2,000 feet AGL.\" Trap: (c) is a true statement about SURFACE winds (45\u00b0 across the isobars, below 2,000 feet AGL), not gradient winds.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.185"},
    {q:"You are standing on the surface in the Northern Hemisphere with the wind at your back. Applying Buys Ballot's Law, where is the area of lower pressure?", o:["Directly behind you, because surface winds blow outward from the low.", "To your right and slightly aft.", "Slightly forward, or directly to your left.", "To your right and slightly forward, because the winds cross the isobars."], a:2, r:"SG: \"This law states that if the wind is at your back, the area of lower pressure will be to your left. When standing on the Earth's surface, the low will be slightly forward or directly left because the winds flow across the isobars.\" Trap: (d) reverses the hemisphere sense \u2014 the law places the low to the LEFT, not the right.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.185"},
    {q:"Which statement best explains why the surface wind differs in direction from the gradient wind above it?", o:["Friction increases wind speed, which strengthens the Coriolis force and turns the wind further to the right.", "Friction reduces wind speed, which reduces the Coriolis force, so the air blows across the isobars from high pressure toward low pressure at an assumed 45\u00b0 angle.", "Friction eliminates the pressure gradient force, leaving only the Coriolis force and friction to be balanced.", "Friction reverses the Coriolis deflection, so surface winds turn to the left of the pressure gradient force."], a:1, r:"SG: \"Surface friction reduces the speed of the wind, which causes a reduction in the Coriolis force... the air blows at an angle across the isobars from high pressure to low pressure... we will assume a 45\u00b0 angle.\" Trap: (a) reverses the effect of friction \u2014 friction reduces, never increases, wind speed and the resulting Coriolis force.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.186"},
    {q:"The jet stream is defined as a narrow band of strong winds of at least how many knots, and what is its average speed?", o:["50 knots or more; averaging about 100 to 150 knots.", "50 knots or more; averaging in excess of 250 knots.", "100 knots or more; averaging about 150 to 250 knots.", "30 knots or more; averaging about 50 to 100 knots."], a:0, r:"SG: \"The jet stream is a narrow band of strong winds of 50 knots or more\"; \"These winds average about 100-150 knots but may reach speeds in excess of 250 knots.\" Trap: (b) uses the correct 50-knot threshold but pairs it with the peak (in excess of 250 knots) rather than the average.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.187"},
    {q:"Which set of dimensions correctly describes a typical jet stream segment?", o:["100 to 400 miles in length, 1,000 to 3,000 miles in width, and 30,000 feet in height.", "3,000 to 7,000 miles in length, 1,000 to 3,000 miles in width, and 100 to 400 feet in height.", "1,000 to 3,000 miles in length, 3,000 to 7,000 miles in width, and 100 to 400 feet in height.", "1,000 to 3,000 miles in length, 100 to 400 miles in width, and 3,000 to 7,000 feet in height."], a:3, r:"SG: \"it is found in segments from 1,000 to 3,000 miles in length, 100 to 400 miles in depth/width, and 3,000 to 7,000 feet in height (from the bottom base altitude to the top altitude).\" Trap: (a) swaps the length and width figures, and its 30,000-foot number is the average height of jet stream winds MSL, not a segment dimension; (c) keeps the correct length but assigns the height range (3,000 to 7,000) to the width and the width range (100 to 400) to the height.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.187"},
    {q:"Which statement correctly describes a sea breeze?", o:["An offshore wind that develops at night because the land cools more rapidly than the water.", "An onshore wind that develops at night, seldom exceeding 5 knots, that penetrates far inland.", "An onshore wind that develops during the day as cool air over the water moves toward the lower pressure over the warm land, with speeds sometimes reaching 15 to 20 knots.", "An onshore daytime wind produced because pressure over the land becomes higher than pressure over the water."], a:2, r:"SG: \"The cool air over the water moves toward the lower pressure, replacing the warm air over the land that moved upward. The resulting onshore wind, blowing from the sea, is called a sea breeze, with speeds sometimes reaching 15 to 20 knots.\" Trap: (a) describes the LAND breeze, which occurs at night and blows offshore.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.188"},
    {q:"Compared with the sea breeze, the land breeze is:", o:["weaker, blows offshore from land to sea, and is shallow in depth.", "stronger, blows onshore from sea to land, and is shallow in depth.", "weaker, blows onshore from sea to land, and penetrates far inland.", "stronger, blows offshore from land to sea, and extends to high altitudes."], a:0, r:"SG: \"At night, the circulation is reversed so that the air movement is from land to sea, producing an offshore wind called the land breeze\"; \"The sea breezes are usually stronger than the land breezes, but they seldom penetrate far inland. Both land and sea breezes are shallow in depth.\" Trap: (d) reverses the relative strength \u2014 the sea breeze, not the land breeze, is the stronger of the two.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.189"},
    {q:"A cloud has a base at 5,000 feet AGL and a top at 8,000 feet AGL. How is it classified, and why?", o:["Middle cloud, because its top is above 6,500 feet AGL.", "Special cloud with extensive vertical development, because it spans two cloud groups.", "Middle cloud, because the average of the base and top heights exceeds 6,500 feet AGL.", "Low cloud, because the height of the cloud base, not the top, determines the classification."], a:3, r:"SG: \"The height of the cloud base, not the top, determines the classification. A cloud with a base at 5000 feet AGL and a top at 8000 feet AGL is classified as a low cloud.\" Trap: (a) uses the cloud top, which is exactly the criterion the guide rules out.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.196"},
    {q:"Which statement correctly describes the middle cloud group?", o:["Bases from just above the surface to 6,500 feet AGL; no special prefix is attached to the name, and they are mainly composed of water droplets.", "Bases between 6,500 and 20,000 feet AGL; their names contain the prefix alto-, and they are composed of ice crystals, water droplets, or a mixture of the two.", "Bases between 6,500 and 20,000 feet AGL; their names contain the prefix cirro-, and they are composed mostly of ice crystals.", "Bases above 20,000 feet AGL; icing is common in them because of super-cooled water droplets."], a:1, r:"SG: \"In this category, cloud bases form between 6,500 and 20,000 feet AGL. The names of the middle clouds will contain the prefix alto- ... They are composed of ice crystals, water droplets, or a mixture of the two.\" Trap: (c) attaches the correct middle-cloud altitude band to the HIGH-cloud prefix (cirro-) and composition.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.196"},
    {q:"Which statement about high clouds is correct?", o:["Being composed mostly of ice crystals, they produce no precipitation and do not constitute an icing hazard.", "Being composed mostly of water droplets, they produce light rain or drizzle and present a serious icing hazard.", "Their names contain the prefix alto-, and their bases average 20,000 to 40,000 feet AGL.", "They present no turbulence hazard of any kind, even in the anvil of a thunderstorm."], a:0, r:"SG: \"Since high clouds are composed mostly of ice crystals, they have no precipitation and do not constitute an icing hazard.\" Trap: (d) overstates the guide \u2014 \"Severe or extreme turbulence is often found in the anvil cirrus of thunderstorms.\"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.196"},
    {q:"Which statement about nimbostratus and the clouds of extensive vertical development is correct?", o:["Nimbostratus produces showery precipitation, and its base seldom descends below 6,500 feet AGL.", "Towering cumulus are the thunderstorm clouds, sometimes referred to as a \"CB,\" and they produce hail and lightning.", "Nimbostratus produces continuous rain, snow, or ice pellets, with a base extending down to about 1,000 feet AGL and fog often present.", "The bases of towering cumulus and cumulonimbus are found in the high cloud category, with their tops in the middle cloud category."], a:2, r:"SG: \"A special cloud, nimbostratus, produces continuous rain, snow, or ice pellets. The cloud base will extend down to about 1,000 ft AGL, and fog is often present.\" Trap: (b) misassigns the \"CB\" label \u2014 \"Cumulonimbus clouds are thunderstorm clouds. A cumulonimbus cloud is sometimes referred to as a 'CB.'\"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.196"},
    {q:"Precipitation with a sudden beginning and ending and abruptly changing intensity is which characteristic, and with which cloud form is it associated?", o:["Continuous; stratiform.", "Intermittent; stratiform only.", "Continuous; cumuliform.", "Showers; cumuliform."], a:3, r:"SG: \"Showers - Characterized by a sudden beginning and ending, and abruptly changing intensity and/or sky conditions. Showers are associated with cumuliform clouds.\" Trap: (a) gives the true description of CONTINUOUS precipitation's cloud association, but the stem describes showers.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.195"},
    {q:"Which statement correctly describes intermittent precipitation?", o:["It stops and restarts at least once during the hour and may be associated with either cumuliform or stratiform clouds.", "It stops and restarts at least once during the hour and is associated only with cumuliform clouds.", "Its intensity changes gradually, if at all, and it is associated with stratiform clouds.", "It is characterized by a sudden beginning and ending and is associated with cumuliform clouds."], a:0, r:"SG: \"Intermittent - Stops and restarts at least once during the hour. Intermittent precipitation may be showery or continuous, and therefore may be associated with cumuliform or stratiform clouds.\" Trap: (b) has the correct timing definition but wrongly restricts it to cumuliform clouds only.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.195"},
    {q:"Parcels of an air mass become displaced upward through four mechanisms. Which list names all four?", o:["Convergence, subsidence, orographic, and thermal.", "Convergence, frontal, orographic, and thermal.", "Divergence, frontal, adiabatic, and convective.", "Convergence, frontal, katabatic, and radiational."], a:1, r:"SG: \"The four methods of lifting are convergence, frontal, orographic, and thermal (Figure 2-13).\" Trap: (a) swaps \"frontal\" for \"subsidence,\" which is sinking rather than lifting.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.199"},
    {q:"Wind pushing air up the side of a mountain, and cool air lying over a warm surface being heated until it rises, are respectively:", o:["convergence lifting and frontal lifting.", "frontal lifting and orographic lifting.", "thermal lifting and orographic lifting.", "orographic lifting and thermal (convective) lifting."], a:3, r:"SG: \"Orographic lifting is a term indicating that the force of the wind against a mountainside pushes the air upward. Thermal lifting, also known as convective lifting, is caused when cool air is over a warm surface, and it is heightened by intense solar heating.\" Trap: (c) contains both correct terms but in reversed order relative to the stem.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.199"},
    {q:"A lifted parcel of air is found to be warmer than the surrounding air. When the lifting action is removed, the parcel will:", o:["settle back toward its original level, indicating a stable condition.", "remain at the level where lifting stopped, indicating a neutrally stable condition.", "continue to rise because it is less dense, indicating an unstable condition and the point of free convection.", "continue to rise because it is denser than the surrounding air, indicating a stable condition."], a:2, r:"SG: \"Lifted air that is warmer than the surrounding air continues to rise when the lifting action is removed because it is less dense... This lifted air that continues to rise has reached the point of free convection.\" Trap: (a) is the true outcome for lifted air that is COLDER than its surroundings.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.200"},
    {q:"Lifted air that ends up with the same temperature as the surrounding air will:", o:["remain at the point where the lifting action was removed, which is a neutrally stable atmosphere.", "settle back to its original position, which is a stable atmosphere.", "continue to rise with no external lifting force, which is an unstable atmosphere.", "descend faster than it was lifted, which is a stable atmosphere."], a:0, r:"SG: \"Lifted air that has the same temperature as the surrounding air after it is lifted will simply remain at the point where the lifting action was removed. This is an example of a neutrally stable atmosphere.\" Trap: (b) applies the stable-air outcome (settling back, because the parcel is denser) to a parcel that is not colder than its surroundings.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.200"},
    {q:"Which group of \"signs in the sky\" all indicate STABLE air?", o:["Dust devils, towering clouds, and rapidly decreasing air temperature while climbing.", "Temperature inversions, low fog and stratus, and rising air temperature while climbing.", "Showers, thunderstorms, and temperature inversions.", "Low fog and stratus, dust devils, and rapidly decreasing air temperature while climbing."], a:1, r:"SG: \"Here are some additional 'signs in the sky' that indicate stable air: temperature inversions, low fog and stratus, and rising air temperature while climbing.\" Trap: (d) mixes one valid stable indicator (low fog and stratus) with two unstable ones (dust devils, rapidly decreasing temperature while climbing).", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.194"},
    {q:"Assuming sufficient moisture for cloud development, cumuliform clouds along your route indicate ______ conditions, while stratiform clouds indicate ______ conditions.", o:["stable / unstable", "neutrally stable / unstable", "unstable / stable", "stable / neutrally stable"], a:2, r:"SG: \"Cumuliform clouds develop with unstable conditions and stratiform clouds develop with stable conditions (Figure 2-14), assuming sufficient moisture exists for cloud development.\" Trap: (a) is the exact reversal of the guide's pairing.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.194"},
    {q:"Which statement best defines a front and identifies its surface position?", o:["A front is the center of a low-pressure system; the surface front is the point of lowest pressure.", "A front is the boundary between two air masses of identical temperature and moisture; the surface front is drawn where the isobars intersect.", "A front is an area of discontinuity within a single air mass; the surface front is the highest point along the frontal slope.", "A front is an area of discontinuity that forms between two contrasting air masses adjacent to each other; the point where the front comes in contact with the ground is the surface front."], a:3, r:"SG: \"A front is an area of discontinuity that forms between two contrasting air masses when they are adjacent to each other\"; \"The point where a front comes in contact with the ground is called the surface front.\" Trap: (b) fails on \"identical temperature and moisture\" \u2014 the guide requires that the air masses have \"sufficiently different temperature and moisture properties.\"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.190"},
    {q:"Which four properties of adjacent air masses are used to locate and classify fronts?", o:["Temperature, moisture (indicated by the dew point), winds, and pressure.", "Temperature, cloud type, precipitation, and visibility.", "Pressure, density altitude, winds, and stability.", "Dew point, temperature, ceiling, and frontal slope."], a:0, r:"SG: \"Differences in the various properties of adjacent air masses\u2013in particular, their temperature, moisture (indicated by the dew point), winds, and pressure\u2013are used to locate and classify fronts.\" Trap: (d) substitutes ceiling and slope; slope is a frontal characteristic discussed in the guide, but it is not one of the four discontinuities.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.191"},
    {q:"As a front approaches and then passes a station in the Northern Hemisphere, what happens to the pressure and the wind?", o:["Pressure rises as the front approaches and falls after passage; the wind shifts counterclockwise.", "Pressure decreases as the front approaches and rises immediately following frontal passage; the wind changes direction in a clockwise rotation.", "Pressure remains steady through the passage; only wind speed changes.", "Pressure decreases both before and after passage; the wind shifts counterclockwise, requiring a left heading correction to hold ground track."], a:1, r:"SG: \"when a front approaches a station, or a pilot flies toward a front, the pressure decreases. Pressure then rises immediately following frontal passage\"; \"as the front approaches and passes a station the wind changes direction in a clockwise rotation.\" Trap: (d) has the pressure trend half right but reverses the wind shift \u2014 the guide says \"you must adjust heading to the right to maintain your original ground track.\"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.191"},
    {q:"On average, a cold front moves toward which direction, at what speed, and with what wind shift?", o:["Toward the NE at 15 knots, with the wind shifting from SE to SW.", "Toward the SE at 15 knots, with the wind shifting from SE to SW.", "Toward the NE at 20 knots, with the wind shifting from SW to NW.", "Toward the SE at 20 knots, with the wind shifting from SW to NW."], a:3, r:"SG: \"Cold fronts move toward the SE at 20kts, on average, and the wind shift is from the SW to the NW.\" Trap: (a) supplies the WARM front's 15-knot speed and SE-to-SW wind shift.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"How does frontal slope relate to the weather a front produces?", o:["Steep slopes produce extensive cloudiness with large areas of steady precipitation, while shallow slopes produce narrow bands of showers.", "Slope has no bearing on the weather produced; only the moisture content of the air masses matters.", "Shallow slopes produce extensive cloudiness with large areas of steady precipitation, while steep slopes tend to move rapidly and produce narrow bands of cloudiness and showery precipitation.", "Shallow slopes normally separate air masses of vastly different properties and therefore indicate the potential for more severe weather."], a:2, r:"SG: \"Shallow frontal slopes tend to produce extensive cloudiness with large areas of steady precipitation, while steep frontal slopes tend to move rapidly producing narrow bands of cloudiness and showery precipitation.\" Trap: (d) borrows a true sentence about STEEP slopes (\"Steep frontal slopes normally separate air masses of vastly different properties\") and attaches it to shallow slopes.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"Which set of characteristics belongs to a warm front?", o:["Average speed of 15 knots, a gradual (shallow) slope, and a wind shift from SE to SW.", "Average speed of 20 knots, a steep slope, and a wind shift from SW to NW.", "Average speed of 15 knots, a steep slope, and a wind shift from SE to NW.", "Little or no movement, with a 180\u00b0 wind shift."], a:0, r:"SG: \"A warm front typically moves at a slower speed than a cold front\u201315 knots on average\u2013and produces a more gradual frontal slope\"; \"The winds shift across a warm front from the SE to the SW.\" Trap: (b) is the cold front triplet (20 knots, steep slope, SW to NW).", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"Approaching an active warm front from the east, what cloud sequence would you expect, and how far ahead of the surface front does the cloud system extend?", o:["Cirrus, altocumulus, cumulonimbus, then towering cumulus; 50 to 300 miles.", "Cirrus, cirrostratus, altostratus, nimbostratus, then stratus with rain and fog; about 500 to 700 miles.", "Stratus, nimbostratus, altostratus, cirrostratus, then cirrus; about 100 to 400 miles.", "Altocumulus followed immediately by cumulonimbus; less than 50 miles."], a:1, r:"SG: \"the sequence of clouds would be cirrus, cirrostratus, altostratus, nimbostratus, and stratus, rain and fog\"; \"This cloud system extends from the front's surface position to about 500 to 700 miles in advance of it.\" Trap: (c) lists the correct cloud family members in reverse order and pairs them with the jet stream's 100-to-400-mile width figure.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"Which statement about a stationary front is correct?", o:["It has a 90\u00b0 wind shift, and its weather resembles that of a fast-moving cold front.", "Because neither air mass is replacing the other, no wind blows on either side of the front.", "Surface winds blow perpendicular into the front from both sides, which is why the wind shift is 180\u00b0.", "Surface winds tend to blow parallel on both sides of the front, giving a 180\u00b0 wind shift, and the weather resembles a warm front but is usually less intense."], a:3, r:"SG: \"Surface winds tend to blow parallel on both sides of the front rather than against and or away from it. Therefore, a stationary front has a 180\u00b0 wind shift\"; \"The weather conditions occurring with a stationary front are similar to those found with the warm front, but are usually less intense.\" Trap: (c) reaches the correct 180\u00b0 figure through the wrong reasoning \u2014 the guide says winds blow parallel to the front, not perpendicular into it.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"Which statement correctly describes an occluded front?", o:["It forms when a faster-moving warm front overtakes a slower-moving cold front, moves toward the SE, and produces a 90\u00b0 wind shift.", "It is named for whichever front lifts off the ground, and the wind shift across it is from SW to NW.", "It forms when a faster-moving cold front overtakes a slower-moving warm front, tends to be aligned NW to SE, moves toward the NE at the speed of the front remaining on the ground, and has a 180\u00b0 wind shift from SE to NW.", "It is aligned NE to SW, moves toward the SE at the speed of the front lifted aloft, and has a 90\u00b0 wind shift."], a:2, r:"SG: \"Occluded fronts form when a faster moving cold front overtakes a slower moving warm front\"; \"Both types of occlusions tend to be aligned from NW to SE, and hence move toward the NE at the speed of the front that remains on the ground\"; \"the wind shift is SE to NW.\" Trap: (b) inverts the naming rule \u2014 \"The type of occlusion that forms depends on which front remains in contact with the ground,\" not which one lifts off it.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"Which statement about squall lines is correct?", o:["They form 500 to 700 miles ahead of a warm front and are plotted as a solid purple line.", "They are lines of violent thunderstorms that develop 50 to 300 miles ahead of the cold front, roughly parallel to it, and are indicated on surface charts by a dashed, double-dotted red line.", "They can only exist in direct contact with a cold front and are plotted as a dashed blue line.", "They develop 50 to 300 miles behind the cold front and dissipate immediately after maximum daytime heating."], a:1, r:"SG: \"A squall line is a line of violent thunderstorms. They are indicated on surface charts by a dashed, double-dotted red line. They develop 50 to 300 miles ahead of the cold front and roughly parallel to it.\" Trap: (c) contradicts the guide's note that \"squall lines can be located nowhere near a cold front, possibly from the convergence of air flows at one location.\"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.193"},
    {q:"Squall lines are usually most intense at what time of day, and what wind shift accompanies them?", o:["Late afternoon and early evening, just after maximum daytime heating; a 90\u00b0 shift from SW to NW.", "Just before sunrise, at the daily temperature minimum; a 90\u00b0 shift from SE to SW.", "Late afternoon and early evening, just after maximum daytime heating; a 180\u00b0 shift from SE to NW.", "Around midday, at maximum solar elevation; no appreciable wind shift."], a:0, r:"SG: \"Squall lines are usually the most intense during the late afternoon and early evening hours, just after maximum daytime heating\"; \"Similar to cold fronts, Squall lines will also have a 90\u00b0 wind shift from the SW to the NW.\" Trap: (c) has the timing right but attaches the 180\u00b0 SE-to-NW shift, which belongs to an occluded front.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.193"},
  ]
},
{
  name: "Topic 3 \u2014 Hazards",
  desc: "Chapter-focused review: turbulence, icing, fog, thunderstorms, and microbursts.",
  questions: [
    {q:"Which of the following is the Student Guide definition of turbulence?", o:["A sudden change in wind speed or direction over a short distance in the atmosphere", "Any irregular or disturbed flow in the atmosphere producing gusts and/or eddies", "A current of air moving contrary to the main current, forming swirls or whirlpools", "An intense, highly localized downward atmospheric flow that diverges outward at the surface"], a:1, r:"SG: \"Turbulence is any irregular or disturbed flow in the atmosphere producing gusts and or eddies.\" Option A is the guide's definition of wind shear, a true statement in the wrong slot.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.202"},
    {q:"How many turbulence intensity levels does the guide list for use in a Pilot Report (PIREP)?", o:["Two", "Three", "Four", "Five"], a:2, r:"SG: \"Table 3-1 contains the four intensity levels and the three time descriptors used by aircrew when giving a Pilot Report (PIREP).\" The tempting \"three\" is the number of time descriptors, not intensity levels.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.203"},
    {q:"Besides intensity, the guide provides descriptors for reporting turbulence with respect to time. How many time descriptors are there, and who applies them?", o:["Two, applied by the forecaster preparing the flight weather briefing", "Three, applied by the ground weather observer", "Four, applied by aircrew giving a PIREP", "Three, applied by aircrew giving a PIREP"], a:3, r:"SG: \"Table 3-1 contains the four intensity levels and the three time descriptors used by aircrew when giving a Pilot Report (PIREP), which details the in-flight weather.\" Option C swaps in the intensity count (four) and option B gives the descriptors to the observer instead of the aircrew.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.204"},
    {q:"The different types of turbulence are divided according to which four causative factors?", o:["Thermal, mechanical, frontal, and orographic", "Thermal, mechanical, frontal, and large-scale wind shear", "Convective, mountain wave, frontal, and clear-air", "Thermal, mechanical, inversion, and microburst"], a:1, r:"SG: \"The different types of turbulence can be divided according to their causative factors: thermal, mechanical, frontal, and large-scale wind shear.\" Orographic lifting is real but it is a lifting method from Chapter 2, not one of the four turbulence categories.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.205"},
    {q:"Which statement about clear-air turbulence (CAT) agrees with the guide?", o:["CAT is found only in the large-scale wind shear category of turbulence.", "CAT normally occurs outside of clouds, usually above 15,000 feet MSL, and can be found in each of the four categories of turbulence.", "CAT occurs only inside cumulonimbus clouds, in the shear between the updraft and the downdraft.", "CAT is limited to the 2,000-foot layer immediately below the tropical tropopause."], a:1, r:"SG: \"CAT normally occurs outside of clouds and usually occurs at altitudes above 15,000 feet MSL... CAT is not limited to jet streams\u2013in fact CAT can be found in each of the four categories of turbulence.\" Option A reverses the guide's statement, which is that \"the Wind Shear category of turbulence is only CAT,\" not that CAT is only wind shear.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.205"},
    {q:"Which surface would generally produce the strongest convective currents, and therefore the strongest thermal turbulence?", o:["A large lake on a hot summer afternoon", "A grass-covered field", "A plowed field or rocky wasteland", "A forested ridgeline"], a:2, r:"SG: \"dry, barren surfaces such as sandy or rocky wasteland and plowed fields absorb heat more readily than surfaces covered with grass or other vegetation... Thus, barren surfaces generally cause stronger convective currents.\" Water is the trap: \"water surfaces are heated more slowly.\"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.206"},
    {q:"Of the factors that govern the strength and vertical extent of mechanical turbulence, which does the guide identify as the most important?", o:["The speed of the wind", "The roughness of the terrain or the nature of the obstruction", "The stability of the air", "The moisture content of the air"], a:2, r:"SG: \"Stability seems to be the most important factor in determining the strength and vertical extent of the mechanical turbulence.\" Wind speed and terrain roughness are both listed as factors, but neither is called the most important.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.207"},
    {q:"The most severe cases of frontal turbulence are generally associated with which front, and where are the greater wind speeds normally found?", o:["Fast-moving cold fronts; wind speeds are normally greater in the cold air mass", "Fast-moving warm fronts; wind speeds are normally greater in the warm air mass", "Slow-moving cold fronts; wind speeds are normally greater in the warm air mass", "Slow-moving warm fronts; wind speeds are normally greater in the cold air mass"], a:0, r:"SG: \"The most severe cases of frontal turbulence are generally associated with fast moving cold fronts... The wind speeds are normally greater in the cold air mass.\" Options B and D reverse the front type and/or put the stronger winds in the warm air mass.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.208"},
    {q:"You are landing in near-calm surface winds under clear skies within a few hours of sunrise. You should expect a shear zone at the inversion if the wind at 2,000 to 4,000 feet AGL is at least:", o:["10 knots", "15 knots", "25 knots", "50 knots"], a:2, r:"SG: \"If the wind at 2000 to 4000 feet AGL is 25 knots or more, expect a shear zone at the inversion.\" Option B (15 knots) is close enough to look right but appears nowhere in the inversion discussion.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.209"},
    {q:"What is the primary hazard of low-level wind shear turbulence?", o:["Structural damage to the airframe from repeated gust loading", "A loss of lift and airspeed near the ground, such as when a headwind becomes a tailwind", "A compressor stall or flameout caused by disturbed inlet airflow", "Erroneous airspeed and altimeter indications caused by rapid pressure fluctuations"], a:1, r:"SG: \"The resulting turbulence can often cause a loss of lift and airspeed near the ground, such as when a headwind becomes a tailwind... the loss of lift can be catastrophic because of your proximity to the ground.\" Compressor stalls and structural damage are true consequences of severe turbulence generally, but they are not the primary low-level wind shear hazard.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.210"},
    {q:"You are in jet stream turbulence and want out of it as quickly as possible. Which action does the guide recommend?", o:["Turn north toward the cold air side, where the horizontal shear is weakest", "Hold altitude and heading; jet stream turbulence patches average 50 miles deep and 20 miles long", "Turn south; if the outside air temperature is falling, climb", "Turn south; if the outside air temperature is falling, descend"], a:3, r:"SG: \"if it is desired to exit jet stream turbulence, a turn to the south should result in smoother air... If the temperature is rising, climb; if the temperature is falling, descend.\" Option C reverses the temperature rule; option B garbles the patch dimensions (2000 feet deep, 20 miles wide, 50 miles long).", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.211"},
    {q:"Which of the following is NOT one of the recommended procedures for flying through turbulence?", o:["Control both pitch and bank by reference to the attitude gyro indicator", "Establish and maintain thrust settings consistent with turbulent air penetration airspeed", "Re-trim as required to hold the assigned altitude and recapture the target airspeed", "Allow altitude to vary; sacrifice altitude to maintain the desired attitude"], a:2, r:"SG: \"Trim the aircraft for level flight at the recommended turbulent air penetration airspeed. Don't change trim after the proper attitude has been established,\" and \"Don't chase the altimeter.\" Option D looks like a violation of good airmanship but is in fact the guide's own instruction (\"Allow altitude to vary\").", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.212"},
    {q:"Clear icing normally occurs in which temperature range and under which conditions?", o:["0\u00b0 C to \u201310\u00b0 C, from large water droplets in unstable air such as cumulus clouds and areas of freezing rain or drizzle", "\u201310\u00b0 C to \u201320\u00b0 C, from small droplets that freeze instantaneously in stratiform clouds", "\u20138\u00b0 C to \u201315\u00b0 C, where both large and small water droplets are present", "0\u00b0 C to \u201320\u00b0 C, from ice crystals deposited on a cold airframe"], a:0, r:"SG: \"Clear icing normally occurs at temperatures between 0\u00b0 C and \u201310\u00b0 C, where water droplets are large because of unstable air, such as in cumulus clouds and in areas of freezing rain or drizzle.\" Option B is the rime-ice temperature band and droplet description offered for a clear-ice question.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.213"},
    {q:"What are the two requirements for the formation of aircraft icing?", o:["Visible moisture and a relative humidity of at least 75 percent", "Super-cooled visible water droplets, plus a free air temperature and an aircraft surface temperature below freezing", "Condensation nuclei, a low dew point spread, and light surface winds", "Super-cooled water droplets and a free air temperature below \u201320\u00b0 C"], a:1, r:"SG: \"First, the atmosphere must have super-cooled visible water droplets. Second, the free air temperature... and the aircraft's surface temperature must be below freezing.\" Option C is a true statement about fog formation requirements, not icing.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.214"},
    {q:"Which cloud temperature range does the guide tell aircrew to avoid as the range most conducive to structural icing?", o:["0\u00b0 C to \u201310\u00b0 C", "\u20138\u00b0 C to \u201315\u00b0 C", "\u201310\u00b0 C to \u201340\u00b0 C", "0\u00b0 C to \u201320\u00b0 C"], a:3, r:"SG: \"Avoid flying in clouds with temperatures from 0\u00b0 C to \u201320\u00b0 C,\" reinforced by \"serious icing is rare in clouds with temperatures below \u201320\u00b0 C.\" Option C (\u201310 to \u201340) is the freezing range of super-cooled droplets, a real number in the wrong slot.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.215"},
    {q:"Which statement correctly summarizes the aerodynamic effects of structural icing?", o:["Lift, thrust, and range decrease while drag, weight, fuel consumption, and stall speed increase", "Lift and thrust increase while drag and stall speed decrease", "Lift and drag both decrease while weight and range increase", "Weight increases, but lift, drag, and stall speed are unaffected until the ice exceeds one half inch"], a:0, r:"SG: \"The presence of ice on an aircraft decreases lift, thrust, and range, and increases drag, weight, fuel consumption, and stall speed.\" Option D is wrong because location, roughness, and shape matter as much as thickness \u2014 \"a half-inch high ridge of ice... at 4% chord reduces maximum lift by over 50%.\"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.216"},
    {q:"Which statement about engine icing agrees with the guide?", o:["Induction icing can occur only inside clouds that contain super-cooled water droplets.", "Compressor icing is first noticed through a rapid drop in exhaust gas temperature and an increase in thrust.", "Ice on inlet screens must accumulate for at least 30 minutes before it is sufficient to cause turbine failure.", "Induction icing is possible in clear skies at free air temperatures of 10\u00b0 C or less with high relative humidity, because reduced pressure at the intake lowers the temperature."], a:3, r:"SG: \"if the free air temperature is 10\u00b0 C or less (especially near the freezing point), and the relative humidity is high, the possibility of induction icing exists.\" Option B reverses the compressor icing symptoms \u2014 the guide says \"a loss of thrust and a rapid rise in exhaust gas temperature\" \u2014 and option C reverses \"in less than 1 minute.\"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.217"},
    {q:"Which of the following is a ground icing hazard identified in the guide?", o:["De-icing fluids are highly corrosive to internal aircraft and engine parts.", "Taxiing through mud, water, or slush can create a covering of ice that hampers the movement of flaps, control surfaces, and the landing gear mechanism.", "Ice and snow on runways affect braking action, which varies widely with aircraft type and weight.", "All of the above"], a:3, r:"SG: \"De-icing fluids... are highly corrosive to internal aircraft and engine parts,\" \"taxiing through mud, water or slush... can create a covering of ice that can hamper the movement of flaps, control surfaces, and the landing gear mechanism,\" and \"Ice and snow on runways are conditions that affect braking action... Braking action varies widely with aircraft type and weight.\" Each option alone is true, which is the trap for anyone who stops reading at A.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 5.6"},
    {q:"Which of the following is a recommended precaution for flying in the vicinity of icing conditions?", o:["When icing is encountered along a front, fly parallel to the front until clear of the weather.", "In freezing rain, descend immediately, since the air is always warmer at lower altitudes.", "Avoid low clouds above mountain ridges or crests, and expect the heaviest icing in clouds around 5,000 feet above the mountaintops.", "Use steep turns to break accumulated ice off the airfoils before landing."], a:2, r:"SG: \"Avoid low clouds above mountain ridges or crests. Expect the heaviest icing in clouds around 5000 feet above the mountaintops.\" Options A and B are exact reversals of guide items 10 and 9 (\"Do not fly parallel to a front while encountering icing conditions\" and \"In freezing rain, climb to temperatures above freezing\").", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.218"},
    {q:"Which set of parameters defines fog?", o:["Based at or within 50 feet of the surface, greater than 20 feet in depth, and reduces prevailing visibility to less than \u215d statute mile", "Based at or within 20 feet of the surface, greater than 50 feet in depth, and reduces prevailing visibility to less than \u215d statute mile", "Based more than 50 feet AGL, greater than 20 feet in depth, and reduces prevailing visibility to less than 1 statute mile", "Based at the surface, greater than 200 feet in depth, and reduces prevailing visibility to less than 3 statute miles"], a:0, r:"SG: \"Fog is a visible aggregate of minute water droplets that is based at or within 50 feet of the surface, is greater than 20 feet in depth, and reduces the prevailing visibility to less than \u215d of a statute mile.\" Option B swaps the 50-foot base and the 20-foot depth \u2014 the classic reversal.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.219"},
    {q:"Obscuring phenomena are best defined as:", o:["Any collection of particles that reduces horizontal visibility to less than 3 miles, always surface based", "Any layer of suspended water droplets based more than 50 feet AGL that does not reduce visibility at the surface", "Any collection of particles, either surface based or aloft, that reduces horizontal visibility to less than 7 miles", "Any collection of particles that reduces prevailing visibility to less than \u215d statute mile"], a:2, r:"SG: \"Obscuring phenomena are any collection of particles that reduce horizontal visibility to less than seven miles. They may be either surface based or aloft.\" Option A borrows the \"less than 3 miles\" figure the guide uses for dense haze.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.220"},
    {q:"Which three conditions must be satisfied for fog to form?", o:["Condensation nuclei must be present, the air must have a high water content (a low dew point spread), and light surface winds must be present", "Condensation nuclei must be present, the air must be unstable, and some form of lifting action must occur", "Super-cooled water droplets must be present, the surface temperature must be below freezing, and the winds must be calm", "A large dew point spread, strong surface winds, and clear skies must be present"], a:0, r:"SG: \"For fog to form, three conditions must be satisfied: (1) condensation nuclei must be present in the air, (2) the air must have a high water content (a low dew point spread), (3) and light surface winds must be present.\" Option B substitutes the thunderstorm-formation ingredients.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.221"},
    {q:"Which surface wind condition will usually produce dense radiation fog?", o:["Less than 5 knots", "5 to 10 knots", "10 to 15 knots", "Greater than 15 knots"], a:1, r:"SG: \"Winds less than 5 knots usually results in shallow fog. Winds of 5 to 10 knots will usually cause dense fog. Winds of greater than 10 knots will usually dissipate the fog.\" Option A produces fog, but shallow fog, not dense.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.222"},
    {q:"Which statement about advection fog agrees with the guide?", o:["It forms over a cold surface at night and normally burns off shortly after sunrise through solar heating.", "It is a type of radiation fog that drains down into valley floors after sunset.", "It requires surface winds of less than 5 knots and remains stationary once it has formed.", "It becomes thicker and denser as wind speed increases up to about 15 knots, and dissipates only with a change in wind direction."], a:3, r:"SG: \"Fog of this type becomes thicker and denser as the wind speed increases, up to about 15 knots,\" and \"Advection fog dissipates only with a wind shift, blowing the fog away.\" Option A is the dissipation behavior of radiation fog, and option B describes valley fog.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.222"},
    {q:"A thunderstorm cell is considered to have transitioned to the mature stage when:", o:["The anvil first becomes visible at the top of the cell", "Precipitation reaches the surface", "The convective updraft first exceeds 3,000 fpm", "Subsiding air replaces the updraft throughout the cloud"], a:1, r:"SG: \"The cell transitions to the mature stage when precipitation reaches the surface.\" Option C describes the towering cumulus stage (\"The strongest updrafts occur at higher altitudes late in the stage and can exceed 3,000 fpm\") and option D describes the dissipating stage.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.223"},
    {q:"Which statement about thunderstorm hazards agrees with the guide?", o:["Hail has been encountered as high as 45,000 feet in completely clear air and may be carried 10 to 20 miles downwind from the storm core.", "Lightning is the most severe hazard associated with a thunderstorm, and it occurs only within the cloud.", "Most of the icing in a thunderstorm occurs in the lower \u2153 of the cell, where the rainfall is heaviest.", "Thunderstorm turbulence is confined to the visible cloud and does not extend above the tops."], a:0, r:"SG: \"Hail has been encountered as high as 45,000 feet in completely clear air and may be carried 10 to 20 miles downwind from the storm core.\" Option C reverses the guide's statement that \"most of the icing... occurs in the top \u2154 of the thunderstorm cell,\" and B ignores that \"Extreme turbulence is the most severe hazard associated with thunderstorms.\"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.223"},
    {q:"Which set of values correctly describes a microburst?", o:["Downward velocities of 2,000 to over 6,000 fpm; differential velocities of 20 to 200 knots; \u00bc to 2\u00bd miles in diameter", "Downward velocities of 200 to 2,000 fpm; differential velocities of 2 to 20 knots; 5 to 10 miles in diameter", "Downward velocities of 2,000 to over 6,000 fpm; differential velocities of 20 to 200 knots; 5 to 20 miles in diameter", "Downward velocities of about 3,000 fpm; differential velocities of 30 to 40 knots; \u00bc to 2\u00bd miles in diameter"], a:0, r:"SG: \"A microburst is an intense, highly localized downward atmospheric flow with velocities of 2000 to over 6000 feet per minute... producing differential velocities ranging from 20 to 200 knots in an area only \u00bc to 2\u00bd miles in diameter.\" Option C keeps the correct velocities but substitutes the gust front distance (5 to 20 miles ahead of the storm) for the microburst diameter.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.224"},
    {q:"Which statement about microburst warning signs and microburst penetration is correct?", o:["Microbursts emanate only from cumulonimbus clouds, so a rain shower without lightning may be disregarded.", "Virga, localized blowing dust in circular or elliptical patterns, rain shafts diverging away from the core of the cell, and roll clouds are all clues to microburst activity.", "The first indication of microburst penetration is a loss of indicated airspeed and a pitch down.", "Once inside the microburst, reduce power and apply nose-down stick force to hold the glidepath."], a:1, r:"SG: \"Visual cues include virga, localized blowing dust (especially in circular or elliptical patterns), rain shafts with rain diverging away from the core of the cell, roll clouds...\" Options C and D invert the penetration sequence and the correct response: the aircraft first sees \"a strong increase in headwind, with a resulting increase in indicated airspeed and lift, which will cause the aircraft to pitch up,\" and the guide warns that reducing power with nose-down stick is exactly the \"natural reaction\" that kills.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.224"},
    {q:"Which statement is correct guidance for avoiding thunderstorm hazards?", o:["Avoid by at least 20 miles any thunderstorm giving an intense, heavy, or extreme radar echo, and fly between such echoes only when they are separated by at least 40 miles.", "Avoid severe echoes by at least 10 miles, and fly between echoes that are separated by at least 20 miles.", "When overflying a storm, clear the tops by 500 feet for every 10 knots of wind speed at the altitude of the tops.", "When flying beneath a storm, select an altitude \u2154 of the distance from the surface to the base of the cloud."], a:0, r:"SG: \"Avoid by at least 20 mi any thunderstorm identified as severe or giving an intense, heavy, or extreme radar echo... Such echoes should be separated by at least 40 mi before flying between echoes.\" Option C halves the over-the-top rule (\"1000 feet for every 10 knots of wind speed at the altitude of the tops\") and option D inverts the \u2153 rule for flying underneath.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.225"},
    {q:"Which statement about rime icing agrees with the guide?", o:["Rime ice forms at \u20138\u00b0 C to \u201315\u00b0 C and is the most frequently encountered form of icing.", "Rime ice spreads over a large portion of the wing surface and is very difficult to break off.", "Mixed icing, not rime icing, forms at \u201310\u00b0 C to \u201320\u00b0 C in stable stratiform clouds from small droplets.", "Rime ice is milky white, is most likely at \u201310\u00b0 C to \u201320\u00b0 C where small droplets freeze instantaneously, and protrudes forward into the airstream along leading edges."], a:3, r:"SG: \"Rime icing is milky white in appearance and is most likely to occur at temperatures of \u201310 to \u201320\u00b0 C... water droplets are small and freeze instantaneously, such as stratiform clouds... Rime ice does not normally spread over an aircraft surface, but protrudes forward into the air stream along the leading edges of airfoils.\" Option A gives rime the mixed-icing temperature band (\u20138 to \u201315\u00b0 C) and the \"most frequent type\" title that belongs to mixed icing, and option B describes clear ice.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.213"},
  ]
},
{
  name: "Topic 4 \u2014 Planning & Resources",
  desc: "Chapter-focused review: visibility, METARs, TAFs, charts, radar, and severe weather products.",
  questions: [
    {q:"Which statement about the types of visibility is correct?", o:["Prevailing visibility must be observed through a continuous half of the horizon circle.", "Flight visibility is the horizontal distance, expressed in hundreds of feet, seen down the runway from the approach end.", "RVR provides the best indication of slant range visibility.", "Slant range visibility is routinely measured from the ground and reported with the prevailing visibility."], a:2, r:"SG: \"Unfortunately, slant rage visibility is not often provided because of great difficulty in estimating or measuring it from the ground. RVR provides the best indication of the slant range visibility.\" Trap distractor (a) is wrong because prevailing visibility is the greatest visibility equaled or exceeded through at least half the horizon circle, \"which need not be continuous.\"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.226"},
    {q:"A ground observer finds the celestial dome totally hidden (8/8) by fog that touches the ground, but she can watch a weather balloon ascend 300 feet into the fog before losing sight of it. Which statement is correct?", o:["There is no ceiling, because a surface-based obscuration cannot produce one.", "There is an \"indefinite\" ceiling of 300 feet, and the 300 feet is reported as the vertical visibility.", "This is a partial obscuration and is reported as BKN000, so no ceiling exists.", "A pilot can expect to see the runway clearly after descending below 300 feet, exactly as with a 300-foot cloud base."], a:1, r:"SG: \"While this does constitute a ceiling, it is sometimes referred to as an 'indefinite' ceiling, and the distance that can be seen upward into the phenomenon is then given as the vertical visibility.\" The SG's own example uses a balloon seen 200 feet up into fog. Trap distractor (d) is wrong: \"a pilot will have difficulty seeing the runway or approach lights clearly even after descending below the level of the reported vertical visibility.\"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.227"},
    {q:"Per the Student Guide, which of the following is NOT a situation in which a pilot is REQUIRED to submit a PIREP?", o:["When a missed approach is executed.", "When wind shear is encountered on departure or arrival.", "When weather conditions on an IFR approach differ from the latest observation.", "When cruising in smooth air with the weather exactly as forecast."], a:3, r:"SG: \"Pilots are required to report when: When weather conditions on an IFR approach differ from the latest observation; When a wind shear is encountered on departure or arrival; In-flight when requested by ATC; When unusual or unforeseen weather conditions are encountered; When a missed approach is executed.\" Routine, as-forecast conditions are not on that required list \u2014 pilots are only \"urged to cooperate and promptly volunteer\" such reports. Trap distractors (a), (b), (c) are all on the required list verbatim.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.228"},
    {q:"An AIRMET issued for areas of moderate airframe icing and for the freezing level is collectively referred to as a:", o:["SIERRA AIRMET.", "TANGO AIRMET.", "ZULU AIRMET.", "Convective SIGMET."], a:2, r:"SG: \"Icing and Freezing Level AIRMETs are ZULU AIRMETs.\" Trap distractor (a) SIERRA is the IFR-conditions/mountain-obscuration AIRMET: \"IFR conditions or Mountain Obscuration AIRMETs are known as SIERRA AIRMETs.\"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.229"},
    {q:"Which statement best describes the use of the Surface Analysis Chart?", o:["It is a forecast of pressure centers and fronts valid for the following 12 hours.", "It depicts observed weather, so the chart represents past history and is not a forecast.", "Its valid time (VT) is the time at which the user loaded the chart.", "Its isobars are drawn at 2-millibar intervals to show the pressure gradient."], a:1, r:"SG: \"The information displayed on the Surface Analysis Chart is observed weather, meaning that the chart represents past history, and is not a forecast.\" Trap distractor (d) is wrong because \"Barometric pressure lines (isobars) are drawn at 4-millibar intervals\" \u2014 not 2.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.18"},
    {q:"A Surface Analysis Chart displays a valid time of 2100Z on 25 October 2023 in the upper left, and 0111Z on 26 October 2023 in the center near the bottom of the image. What do these two times represent?", o:["The observations were gathered on the 25th at 2100Z, and the chart was loaded on the 26th at 0111Z.", "The chart becomes valid on the 26th at 0111Z and expires on the 25th at 2100Z.", "The chart was loaded on the 25th at 2100Z and forecasts conditions for the 26th at 0111Z.", "Both times are local times and mark the interval at which the isobars were re-analyzed."], a:0, r:"SG: \"The valid time (VT) of the chart is the observation time of the information that was gathered to compile the chart and is given in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) at the top left... The time the chart was loaded is displayed in the center near the bottom of the image.\" The SG uses this exact example: data gathered 25 October 2023 at 2100Z, accessed at 0111Z on the 26th. Trap distractor (c) reverses the two times.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.19"},
    {q:"Which statement about Prognostic Charts is correct?", o:["They display only observed weather and never a forecast.", "They are limited to a 12-hour forecast period.", "They are obtained from the 26th OWS website by clicking the \"Radar SCNTRL\" link.", "They include the latest surface analysis along with multiple forecasts for up to a week, depicting predicted positions of fronts and pressure centers."], a:3, r:"SG: \"These charts include the latest surface analysis along with multiple forecasts for up to a week. These forecasts depict predicted positions of fronts and pressure centers, as well as forecast weather across the country.\" Trap distractor (c) is wrong because Prog Charts come from \"the Prog Charts section of the NOAA Aviation Digital Data Service (ADDS) site\"; the \"Radar SCNTRL\" link is the 26th OWS NEXRAD source.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.20"},
    {q:"A station model is plotted with pressure displayed in millibars. The three digits on the upper right read 984, and directly below them is -32. This indicates:", o:["sea level pressure of 998.4 millibars, with the pressure having fallen over the last 3 hours.", "sea level pressure of 1098.4 millibars, with the pressure having risen over the last 3 hours.", "sea level pressure of 984.0 millibars, with the pressure change measured over the last hour.", "an altimeter setting of 29.84 inches of mercury, with the pressure having risen."], a:0, r:"Decode: 984 = SLP in millibars \u2014 SG: \"since SLP will always be somewhere around 1000 millibars, the hundreds digit (and thousands, if present) is dropped, and the decimal point is also omitted. Thus, depicted pressures beginning with large numbers (such as a 9) really start with a hidden '9'.\" So 984 \u2192 998.4 mb. -32 = SG: \"Below the current SLP is the pressure tendency over the last 3 hours, beginning with a (+) or (-) sign to denote an overall rise or fall.\" The minus sign = falling. Trap distractor (d) mis-reads the millibar plot as an inches-of-mercury plot, which would require a leading 2 or 3.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.21"},
    {q:"A station model is plotted with the website set to inches of mercury. The center symbol is a square, and the three digits on the upper right read 998. This station model depicts:", o:["a manned station reporting an altimeter setting of 29.98 inches of mercury.", "an automated station reporting a sea level pressure of 998 millibars.", "an automated station reporting an altimeter setting of 29.98 inches of mercury.", "a manned station reporting an altimeter setting of 39.98 inches of mercury."], a:2, r:"Decode: square center symbol \u2014 SG: \"This model begins with a circle (or a square for automated stations) at the center to represent the location of the station.\" Upper-right 998 with the site set to inches \u2014 SG: \"For inches of mercury, either a 2 or 3 will be placed in front of the numbers show, with a decimal place one from the left digit. Above, the altimeter setting is 29.98 inches of mercury.\" Trap distractor (b) reads the same three digits as a millibar value, but in millibar mode 998 would decode to 999.8 mb, not 998.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.21"},
    {q:"METARs are used in flight planning primarily to:", o:["obtain the forecast ceiling and visibility for the 2-hour arrival window at the destination.", "determine the existing weather at the destination or alternate and whether the field is operating IFR or VFR.", "predict the positions of fronts and pressure centers up to a week in advance.", "forecast the base and thickness of icing and turbulence layers along the route."], a:1, r:"SG: \"METARs are used to communicate the latest observed weather to meteorologists and aircrew so they can determine the existing weather at the destination or alternate, and whether a field is operating under conditions of instrument flight rules (IFR) or visual flight rules (VFR).\" Trap distractor (a) describes the TAF and the 2-hour ETA window technique, not the METAR.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.22"},
    {q:"Refer to the following report:\nMETAR KMOB 141855Z 24012G26KT 1/2SM R05L/1400V2200FT -RA BR SCT000 BKN004 OVC015 18/17 A2984 RMK BR SCT000\nWhat is the ceiling in this report?", o:["There is no ceiling; SCT000 is a surface-based partial obscuration.", "1400 feet AGL.", "1500 feet AGL.", "400 feet AGL."], a:3, r:"Decode the sky condition group by group: SCT000 = surface-based partial obscuration; BKN004 = broken at 400 feet AGL; OVC015 = overcast at 1500 feet AGL. SG: \"a ceiling is the height above the ground (AGL) ascribed to the lowest broken or overcast layer,\" so the lowest BKN/OVC layer \u2014 BKN004 \u2014 is the ceiling at 400 feet. SG: \"Remember that partial obscurations, such as FEW000, or SCT000, do not constitute a ceiling.\" Trap distractor (b) grabs 1400 from the RVR group instead of the cloud group.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.23"},
    {q:"Refer to the following report:\nMETAR KMOB 141855Z 24012G26KT 1/2SM R05L/1400V2200FT -RA BR SCT000 BKN004 OVC015 18/17 A2984 RMK BR SCT000\nThe group R05L/1400V2200FT decodes as:", o:["Runway 05 Left, runway visual range variable between 1400 and 2200 feet.", "Runway 05, left-side RVR 1400 feet and center RVR 2200 feet.", "Runway 05 Left, runway visual range less than 1400 feet.", "Runway 51, runway visual range 1400 to 2200 meters."], a:0, r:"Decode element by element: R = runway; 05 = runway number; L = left; /1400V2200 = two reportable values separated by a V; FT = feet. SG: \"If RVR is varying, the coding will be the same as above, except the two reportable values will be separated by a 'V'\" and the SG example \"R09/1000V4000FT \u2014 Runway 09 visual range 1000 feet variable to 4,000 feet.\" Trap distractor (c) is the \"M\" (Minus) case: \"If RVR is less than its lowest reportable value, the 4-digit value will be preceded with an 'M' (for Minus)\" \u2014 there is no M here.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.23"},
    {q:"Decode the wind information in the following report:  METAR KNGP 032155Z AUTO 33018G31KT 300V010 10SM FEW035 SCT120 31/23 A2996 RMK AO2", o:["Wind from 018 degrees true at 33 knots, varying between 300 and 010 degrees.", "Wind from 330 degrees true at 18 knots with gusts to 31 knots, with the direction varying between 300 and 010 degrees.", "Wind from 330 degrees magnetic at 18 mph gusting to 31 mph, varying between 300 and 010 degrees.", "Wind from 330 degrees at 18 knots, with a peak gust of 31 knots that occurred between minutes 300 and 010."], a:1, r:"Decode: 330 = direction in tens of degrees true; 18 = speed in whole knots; G31 = gusts; KT = knots; 300V010 = variable direction group. SG: \"14015G28KT \u2014 Wind from 140 degrees at 15 knots with gusts to 28 knots\" and \"22015KT 180V250 \u2014 Winds from 220 degrees at 15 knots with direction varying from 180 degrees to 250 degrees.\" SG also states winds are \"in knots and degrees true.\" Trap distractor (c) converts to magnetic and mph; the SG's wind group is always true degrees and knots (ending in KT).", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.23"},
    {q:"Which choice correctly decodes the following report?:  METAR KGPT 120453Z AUTO 00000KT M1/4SM FG VV002 M02/M03 A3011 RMK AO2", o:["Wind information missing, visibility 1/4 SM, cloud base 200 feet AGL, temperature 2\u00b0C and dew point 3\u00b0C.", "Winds calm, visibility 1/4 SM or greater, sky clear, temperature -2\u00b0C and dew point -3\u00b0C.", "Winds calm, visibility less than 1/4 SM, vertical visibility 2000 feet AGL, temperature 2\u00b0C and dew point 3\u00b0C.", "Winds calm, visibility less than 1/4 SM, sky obscured with an indefinite ceiling and a vertical visibility of 200 feet AGL, temperature -2\u00b0C and dew point -3\u00b0C."], a:3, r:"Decode: 00000KT \u2014 SG: \"CALM WINDS \u2014 Calm winds are reported as 00000KT.\" M1/4SM \u2014 SG: \"M1/4 \u2014 Visibility from an automated station less than one-quarter statute mile.\" VV002 \u2014 SG: \"VV008 \u2014 Sky obscured, indefinite ceiling, vertical visibility 800 feet AGL,\" so VV002 = 200 feet. M02/M03 \u2014 SG: \"Sub-zero temperatures or dew points will be prefixed with the letter 'M' (for Minus).\" Trap distractor (c) treats VV002 as 2000 feet and drops the \"M\" on the temperatures.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.23"},
    {q:"Which statement about the following report is correct?:  METAR KNBG 221756Z 09006KT 4SM +TSRA BKN025CB OVC060 26/23 A2989", o:["The altimeter setting is 2989 millibars, and \"heavy\" describes the thunderstorm.", "The ceiling is 6000 feet AGL.", "The altimeter setting is 29.89 inches of mercury, and the \"heavy\" intensity applies to the rain, not to the thunderstorm.", "The thunderstorm is 5 to 10 miles from the point of observation."], a:2, r:"Decode: A2989 \u2014 SG: \"For example, A2992 indicates an altimeter setting of 29.92 inches of Hg.\" +TSRA \u2014 SG present weather convention: \"B. Intensity refers to the precipitation, not its descriptor (TS or SH),\" so the \"+\" describes heavy RAin, not a \"heavy thunderstorm.\" Trap distractor (b) is wrong because the ceiling is the LOWEST broken or overcast layer \u2014 BKN025CB at 2500 feet, not OVC060 \u2014 and (d) is wrong because there is no \"VC\": \"If the letters 'VC' are used, the weather is in the vicinity of 5-10 miles.\"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.23"},
    {q:"Per the Student Guide, the TAF aids the aviator in all of the following EXCEPT:", o:["planning the type of flight (IFR or VFR).", "determining the type of approach that will be required.", "determining the weather currently being observed at the destination.", "determining whether an alternate is required, and selecting the best alternate."], a:2, r:"SG: \"This teletype information will also aid you in planning for the type of flight (IFR/VFR), type of approach you require, determining if an alternate is required, and selection of the best alternate.\" Trap distractor (c) is the METAR's job: \"Previously we learned the Aviation Routine Weather Report (METAR) provides existing weather.\"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.24"},
    {q:"Refer to the following U.S. military TAF:\nKNSE TAF 1512 / 1612 31006KT 8000 HZ SCT015 BKN030 QNH2994INS\nFM151800 34012G22KT 9999 SCT025 BKN060 641005 QNH2991INS\nBECMG 1520 / 1522 02015KT 4800 BR OVC008 QNH2988INS\nTEMPO 1601 / 1605 VRB18G30KT 1600 TSRA BKN010CB\nFM160600 05008KT 9999 SKC QNH2996INS 18/12Z\nWhat is the forecast period covered by the FIRST line of this TAF?", o:["1200Z on the 15th up to, but not including, 1800Z on the 15th.", "1200Z on the 15th up to, but not including, 1200Z on the 16th.", "1200Z on the 15th up to, but not including, 2000Z on the 15th.", "1800Z on the 15th up to, but not including, 1200Z on the 16th."], a:0, r:"Decode: 1512 / 1612 = start the 15th at 1200Z, expire the 16th at 1200Z (the whole 24-hour TAF), and 1512 is also the FROM time of line 1. The TO time of line 1 is the start of the next line, FM151800 = the 15th at 1800Z. SG: \"1st line \u2014 Forecast for NAS Whiting field (KNSE) beginning on the 26th at 0900Z (2609) and valid up to but not including the 26th at 1200Z on the second line (FM261200).\" Trap distractor (b) gives the period of the entire TAF instead of the first line.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.25"},
    {q:"Refer to the following U.S. military TAF:\nKNSE TAF 1512 / 1612 31006KT 8000 HZ SCT015 BKN030 QNH2994INS\nFM151800 34012G22KT 9999 SCT025 BKN060 641005 QNH2991INS\nBECMG 1520 / 1522 02015KT 4800 BR OVC008 QNH2988INS\nTEMPO 1601 / 1605 VRB18G30KT 1600 TSRA BKN010CB\nFM160600 05008KT 9999 SKC QNH2996INS 18/12Z\nThe visibility group 8000 HZ on the first line, followed by 9999 on the FM151800 line, forecasts:", o:["8000 feet of visibility in haze, later improving to 9999 feet.", "8 statute miles of visibility in haze, later improving to 9999 statute miles.", "8000 meters of visibility in hail, later improving to unlimited.", "8000 meters (5 miles) of visibility in haze, later improving to 7 miles or greater, i.e., unlimited visibility."], a:3, r:"Decode: 8000 meters \u2014 the SG decodes this exact value: \"visibility 5 miles (8000 meters).\" HZ is required because \"Whenever the prevailing visibility is forecasted to be 9000 meters or less (6 miles or less) the weather or obstructions to vision causing the reduced visibility will be included.\" 9999 \u2014 SG: \"A visibility code of '9999' indicates 7 miles visibility or greater is forecast, i.e. unlimited visibility.\" Trap distractors (a) and (b) use feet and statute miles; SG: \"For TAFs, forecasted prevailing visibility is reported in meters.\"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.25"},
    {q:"Refer to the following U.S. military TAF:\nKNSE TAF 1512 / 1612 31006KT 8000 HZ SCT015 BKN030 QNH2994INS\nFM151800 34012G22KT 9999 SCT025 BKN060 641005 QNH2991INS\nBECMG 1520 / 1522 02015KT 4800 BR OVC008 QNH2988INS\nTEMPO 1601 / 1605 VRB18G30KT 1600 TSRA BKN010CB\nFM160600 05008KT 9999 SKC QNH2996INS 18/12Z\nThe group 641005 on the FM151800 line forecasts:", o:["moderate turbulence from 10,000 feet AGL to 15,000 feet AGL.", "moderate icing with the base of the stratum at 10,000 feet AGL and the top at 15,000 feet AGL.", "moderate icing from 1000 feet AGL up to 6000 feet AGL.", "severe icing, 10,000 feet thick, beginning at 5000 feet AGL."], a:1, r:"Decode digit by digit against the SG's worked example of 641104: \"The '6' indicates that icing is forecasted. The next digit, '4,' is the type of forecasted icing from Table 4-7 (moderate icing)... The next three digits, '110,' indicate the height of the base of the icing stratum in hundreds of feet AGL, which is 11,000' AGL in this case... The last digit, '4,' is the thickness of the icing layer in thousands of feet (4000' here).\" So 641005 = moderate icing, base 100 \u2192 10,000 feet AGL, thickness 5 \u2192 5000 feet, top 15,000 feet AGL. Trap distractor (a) reads it as turbulence; SG: \"The turbulence group, however, begins with a '5'.\"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.25"},
    {q:"Refer to the following U.S. military TAF:\nKNSE TAF 1512 / 1612 31006KT 8000 HZ SCT015 BKN030 QNH2994INS\nFM151800 34012G22KT 9999 SCT025 BKN060 641005 QNH2991INS\nBECMG 1520 / 1522 02015KT 4800 BR OVC008 QNH2988INS\nTEMPO 1601 / 1605 VRB18G30KT 1600 TSRA BKN010CB\nFM160600 05008KT 9999 SKC QNH2996INS 18/12Z\nThe TEMPO 1601 / 1605 line contains no QNH group. Why?", o:["TEMPO groups do not forecast the QNH group.", "The altimeter group is omitted whenever the setting is forecast to be below 29.92 inches.", "USN/USMC stations never forecast an altimeter setting.", "The QNH group is omitted whenever the visibility is forecast below 1600 meters."], a:0, r:"SG: \"This group forecasts the lowest expected altimeter setting in inches of Hg (Mercury) during the initial forecast period and each subsequent BECMG and FM group (to be discussed shortly) that follows. TEMPO groups (also to be discussed shortly) do not forecast the QNH group.\" Trap distractor (c) confuses the military QNH rule with the civil one: \"U.S. civil stations generally will not forecast an altimeter setting.\"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.25"},
    {q:"Refer to the following U.S. military TAF:\nKNSE TAF 1512 / 1612 31006KT 8000 HZ SCT015 BKN030 QNH2994INS\nFM151800 34012G22KT 9999 SCT025 BKN060 641005 QNH2991INS\nBECMG 1520 / 1522 02015KT 4800 BR OVC008 QNH2988INS\nTEMPO 1601 / 1605 VRB18G30KT 1600 TSRA BKN010CB\nFM160600 05008KT 9999 SKC QNH2996INS 18/12Z\nAn aircraft has an ETA at KNSE of 2015Z on the 15th. Using the Student Guide's flight planning technique, what ceiling should the crew plan for?", o:["2500 feet AGL.", "6000 feet AGL.", "800 feet AGL.", "1000 feet AGL."], a:2, r:"SG: \"an aviator must consider the worst weather conditions that fall within the period of 1 hour prior to the planned estimated time of arrival (ETA) up to but not including 1 hour after ETA, for a total of a 2-hour window.\" ETA 2015Z gives a window of 1915\u20132115Z. The FM151800 line is good up to 2200Z (\"the time indicated by the second two digits of the second 4-digit group are the ending time of the previous line of code\"), and the BECMG 1520 / 1522 line begins at 2000Z (\"The time indicated by the second two digits of the 4-digit group following BECMG will be the beginning time of the new forecast elements\"). So lines 2 and 3 both apply: ceilings BKN060 (6000 feet) and OVC008 (800 feet). SG: build \"a set of the worst-case scenario for each group: strongest winds, lowest visibility, worst weather, lowest ceiling, and lowest altimeter\" \u2014 lowest ceiling is 800 feet AGL. Trap distractor (d) uses BKN010CB from the TEMPO line, which does not begin until 0100Z on the 16th, well outside the arrival window.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.25"},
    {q:"Regarding the display of METARs on the ADDS website, which statement is correct?", o:["The translated option is recommended, because it eliminates the chance of a decoding error by the aircrew.", "The \"Raw\" option is recommended, because a translation may contain errors and omit portions of the raw METAR.", "Graphical METAR plots use the TAF change groups rather than station models.", "METARs are available in textual form only; the graphical plot is a TAF product."], a:1, r:"SG: \"To obtain the report for a particular station, enter the station identifier in the box, check the 'Raw' and 'METARs' checkboxes, and click the Submit button. The translated option is NOT recommended because there is an opportunity for the translation to contain errors and omit portions of the Raw METAR.\" Trap distractor (c) is wrong because graphical METARs use station models: \"Reporting stations are depicted on the chart using station models discussed in earlier lessons.\"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.26"},
    {q:"On a textual Winds-Aloft Forecast, the entry for the 18,000-foot level reads 3125-08. This decodes as:", o:["wind from 031 degrees true at 25 knots, temperature -8\u00b0C.", "wind from 310 degrees magnetic at 25 mph, temperature -8\u00b0F.", "wind from 310 degrees true at 125 knots, temperature -8\u00b0C.", "wind from 310 degrees true at 25 knots, temperature -8\u00b0C."], a:3, r:"Decode: 31 = direction to the nearest ten degrees TRUE (310\u00b0); 25 = speed in knots; -08 = temperature in Celsius. SG: \"The first two represent the true wind direction to the nearest ten degrees true, and the last two digits represent the speed in knots\" and \"2123+04 indicates the wind will be 210\u00b0 T at 23 knots with a temperature of +4\u00b0 Celsius.\" Trap distractor (b) converts to magnetic/mph/Fahrenheit \u2014 winds aloft are true degrees, knots, and Celsius.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.27"},
    {q:"A Winds-Aloft Forecast lists the wind group 7712 for the 39,000-foot level. This decodes as a wind from:", o:["270 degrees true at 112 knots.", "770 degrees true at 12 knots.", "270 degrees true at 12 knots.", "220 degrees true at 112 knots."], a:0, r:"Decode: 77 is an \"unrealistic\" direction (greater than 360\u00b0), so the special high-wind rule applies. SG: \"The wind was encoded by adding 500\u00b0 to the direction and subtracting 100 knots from the speed... To decode such winds, then, one must subtract 500 from 'unrealistic' direction codes and add 100 to the indicated speed.\" 770 \u2212 500 \u2192 270\u00b0T; 12 + 100 \u2192 112 knots. Trap distractor (c) ignores the rule entirely and reads 7712 as a \"normal\" group.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.27"},
    {q:"A Winds-Aloft Forecast lists 9900 at the 6000-foot level, and at the 3000-foot level a wind group appears with no temperature following it. Which statement is correct?", o:["9900 means the winds are calm, and the temperature at 3000 feet was omitted in error.", "9900 means wind from 099 degrees at 0 knots, and temperature is never forecast within 1500 feet of the surface.", "9900 means light and variable winds (forecast speeds less than 5 knots), and temperature is never forecast for the 3000-foot level.", "9900 must be decoded by subtracting 500 from the direction and adding 100 to the speed, giving 490 degrees at 100 knots."], a:2, r:"SG: \"A direction of '99' indicates a variable wind direction. When forecast wind speeds are less than 5 knots, direction is difficult to determine, and the winds are called 'light and variable,' and the code '9900' will be listed.\" And: \"Temperature information is never forecast for the 3000-foot level.\" Trap distractor (b) cites the wind omission rule (\"Wind information is never forecast for altitudes within 1500 feet of the surface\") as if it governed temperature.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.27"},
    {q:"Which statement about ground based weather radar (NEXRAD) is correct?", o:["NEXRAD directly observes tornadic circulation and displays the tornado's position.", "NEXRAD does not directly observe tornadic circulation, but it can display a \"hook echo\" that is considered indicative of a tornado.", "Because of the strong density gradient of the descending air, microbursts are reliably detected out to 100 NM from the radar site.", "Reflectivity values over 25 dbz indicate that precipitation is in the form of hail."], a:1, r:"SG: \"The NEXRAD system does not directly observe tornadic circulation; however, the system can display what is referred to as a 'hook echo' that is considered indicative of a tornado.\" Trap distractor (c) inflates the microburst range; SG: \"because of the shallow vertical extent of the outflow from a microburst, the phenomenon will usually not be detected beyond a range of 20 NM from the radar site.\"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.28"},
    {q:"Which statement correctly distinguishes the two types of satellite imagery?", o:["Infrared imagery records reflected sunlight, while visible imagery records emitted heat radiation.", "Visible imagery is used primarily to determine the temperature of cloud tops.", "Only visible imagery is available for general-purpose use.", "Infrared imagery records the heat radiation emitted by the clouds and the earth and shows temperature differences between cloud tops and the ground."], a:3, r:"SG: \"The IR picture records heat radiation being emitted by the clouds and earth. The images show temperature differences between cloud tops and the ground, as well as temperature gradations of cloud tops over the surface of the Earth.\" Trap distractor (b) misassigns the visible product's purpose; SG: \"the visible picture is primarily used to determine the presence of clouds and the type of clouds from shape and texture.\"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.290"},
    {q:"The National Weather Service defines a SEVERE thunderstorm as any storm producing one or more of the following:", o:["hail at least 1 inch in diameter, and/or wind gusts of 58 mph (50 knots) or greater, and/or a tornado.", "hail at least 2 inches in diameter, and/or wind gusts of 75 mph (65 knots) or greater, and/or a tornado producing EF2 or greater damage.", "any lightning within 5 miles of the field, and/or wind gusts of 30 knots or greater.", "a line of thunderstorms at least 60 miles long with thunderstorms affecting 40% of its length."], a:0, r:"SG: \"A severe thunderstorm refers to a thunderstorm producing hail that is at least 1 inch in diameter or larger, and/or wind gusts of 58 mph or greater, and/or a tornado.\" Trap distractor (b) is the SPC's significant severe thunderstorm definition: \"hail that is at least 2 inches in diameter or larger, and/or wind gusts of 75 mph or greater, and/or a tornado that produces EF2 or greater damage.\"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.30"},
    {q:"Which statement about the DD Form 175-1 (\"Dash One\") is correct?", o:["It is prepared by the pilot and submitted to the local weather office prior to flight.", "Every block on the form will always be completed by the forecaster.", "It is prepared and used by the local weather office to brief pilots on weather locally and along the planned route, and it may be faxed to the pilot if a verbal briefing is not desired.", "It replaces the requirement to review METARs and TAFs for the destination."], a:2, r:"SG: \"The DD Form 175-1, Flight Weather Briefing, is prepared and used by the local weather office to brief pilots on weather conditions both locally and along a planned route of flight. The form may be faxed to you by the weather office if a verbal briefing is not desired.\" Trap distractor (b) is wrong because \"some of the blocks on the form may not be completed, or extra data may be included as an attachment.\"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.31"},
    {q:"Which pair of statements reflects the Student Guide's requirements for preflight weather planning?", o:["The aviator needs only the destination's current METAR, and severe weather is checked last, after the route is built.", "The aviator needs only the forecast, since METARs are historical, and severe weather is the weather office's concern alone.", "The aviator needs only a completed DD Form 175-1, and no separate check for severe weather is required.", "The aviator should know both the destination's existing and forecasted weather, and checking for any severe weather should be the very first step of the flight planning routine."], a:3, r:"SG: \"Any aviator planning a flight should know both the destination's existing and forecasted weather\" and \"When pilots begin their flight planning routine, checking for any severe weather should be the very first step.\" Trap distractor (a) inverts the SG's sequencing \u2014 severe weather is checked first, not last, precisely because \"Changes to missions are a commonplace occurrence due to quickly changing weather conditions.\"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.32"},
  ]
},
];
